WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.660
Tim Ferriss: So Shane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:22.080
there are a million and one places we could start.&nbsp;
So my understanding is you were a straight D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.600
student till grade 10, if I'm getting that right.&nbsp;
And maybe this is a segue, maybe it isn't, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.300
why was the book The Stopwatch Gang&nbsp;
important to you and could you provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.000
some context for how that appeared in your life?
Shane Parrish: So my parents were in the military.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.740
I lived with my mom and my stepfather, my&nbsp;
biological father lived somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.200
and we moved every year. So every year I had&nbsp;
a new school. I think the first school that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.760
went to two years in a row was grade 10 and&nbsp;
11, to put things in context, same school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:01.200
two years in a row. For grade eight, I decided&nbsp;
I was going to live with my biological father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.740
So I moved across the country, lived with&nbsp;
him, and for various reasons, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:18.360
a disaster. Somebody who really didn't have a&nbsp;
lot of experience parenting me growing up all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.060
of a sudden has a teenager, my stepmother all of a&nbsp;
sudden inheriting a teenager, living in the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.220
of nowhere. I'm already a borderline delinquent.&nbsp;
I almost got kicked out of school in grade seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.920
for skipping school. I skipped school for seven or&nbsp;
eight weeks, had to go see a child psychologist to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.560
make sure, as a condition of not being expelled,&nbsp;
that there was nothing seriously wrong with me.&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.940
And so in grade eight, I just started getting&nbsp;
in more and more trouble. I started hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:50.520
around the wrong people. Your environment sort&nbsp;
of determines your behavior a lot, not only your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:56.520
physical environment, but the people you surround&nbsp;
yourself with. And it's just the slow escalation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.480
And if somebody said, "Hey, you're going to go&nbsp;
break into somebody's house," you'd never do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.360
it. But if you start with vandalism, and you start&nbsp;
staying out late, and you start doing things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.880
you're not supposed to, all of a sudden vandalism&nbsp;
or breaking into a house is the next logical step.&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:18.240
So we're doing this book report for&nbsp;
grade eight, and I go to the library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.920
and I'm not interested in reading, I'm not&nbsp;
even interested in doing my schoolwork at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.020
And I just happened to land in this true&nbsp;
crime section at this elementary school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.580
which is like four books long. And there's one&nbsp;
book called The Stopwatch Gang. And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.300
what's this about? I pick it up and it's about&nbsp;
a bunch of bank robbers. And I was like, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:41.400
this is awesome. This is my kind of book. Why&nbsp;
has nobody shown me that you can read about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.980
this stuff? So I get home, and I'm reading this&nbsp;
book, and a few days later, my friends knock on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:52.740
the door. And we all had dirt bikes. So we're all&nbsp;
living on 20 acres. You're commuting by dirt bike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.300
to your friend's house. They knock on the door —
Tim Ferriss: And where are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.007
you at this point in time?
Shane Parrish: This is in Carp, Ontario, Canada.&nbsp;

00:02:58.007 --> 00:03:00.120
Tim Ferriss: Got it. Okay.&nbsp;
Just to place it for people.&nbsp;

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.800
Shane Parrish: So they knock on the door,&nbsp;
and they're like, "Come out with us and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.280
let's have some fun." And I was one chapter away&nbsp;
from the end, and I don't know, you've probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.560
read a really good book, and you want nothing&nbsp;
to interrupt you in that moment. You're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.760
no, the world doesn't exist. I need to finish&nbsp;
this book. There's nothing more important. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.340
I lied because if I told them I was reading a book&nbsp;
that would go over a lead balloon, but I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.300
"I'm not really feeling that well tonight. I'm&nbsp;
going to stay in. I'm sorry about that." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.780
so they go out, and they end up breaking into&nbsp;
somebody's house and getting caught and a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.720
bunch of bad things happened as a result of that.&nbsp;
And if I was there, I probably would've been doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.840
that with them, but luckily I wasn't there.
I didn't do that with them. I found out about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.060
what happened, and this is a chance event. This&nbsp;
wasn't me thinking about anything and saying no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.580
to this thing. It was just pure 100 percent&nbsp;
luck. But I sort of got a little bit scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.560
straight after that, not in terms of "I'm going&nbsp;
to devote myself to schoolwork and get better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.140
grades," but straight in the sense of "I don't&nbsp;
want to do this. I don't want to get into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:15.600
trouble." And then from that moment on, books sort&nbsp;
of became an outlet for me to live in an imaginary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.460
world and to escape the world I was actually&nbsp;
in and to sort of be part of something else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:24.060
Have you ever had a book do that to you?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, for sure. I literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.700
yesterday, this is not something I should&nbsp;
probably advertise broadly. I'm not proud of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:37.500
but I was listening to the second of three&nbsp;
books in this trilogy by Joe Abercrombie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:47.280
and I had six minutes left in this very, very good&nbsp;
chapter, and my mom had arrived a little bit early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.100
to go to dinner. And so I was banking on having&nbsp;
that extra time before the pickup to finish this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.620
chapter. And it bothered me so much that I got&nbsp;
up, I said I really had to go to the bathroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.920
and I went to the bathroom, and I just&nbsp;
walked around outside the restaurant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.800
for six minutes and finished this chapter in&nbsp;
the audiobook. That was last night. So yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.620
I definitely know the feeling.
Shane Parrish: That's awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.140
And you're like, "How dare you show up early?"
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, which is not the tone I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:22.800
take with my mom, but I was like, oh, okay, yeah,&nbsp;
I forgot my OCD as it applies to time should not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:29.580
be imposed on all other people, so that's okay.&nbsp;
Took a little loitering meander as a bathroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.440
break at dinner. That's okay. Got it done.
Shane Parrish: And then in grade nine, my grade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.900
nine teacher wrote in my report card that "Shane&nbsp;
would be lucky to graduate from high school."&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.460
Tim Ferriss: So what happened? A couple&nbsp;
of observations. Number one, if you had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:48.480
been convicted of, I'm not sure what the legal&nbsp;
equivalent is, something like a felony or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.760
that was with your friends, that certainly, I&nbsp;
would imagine, would've complicated later work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.907
in government on some level.
Shane Parrish: Oh, totally.&nbsp;

00:05:55.907 --> 00:06:00.180
Tim Ferriss: Or it could have. What were&nbsp;
the inflection points that led to you being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:06.540
employable, especially at an&nbsp;
intelligence agency, in 2001?&nbsp;

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:14.160
Shane Parrish: Okay, so that is also a crazy story&nbsp;
because intelligence agencies, they vet people up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.020
down. They interviewed neighbors, they interviewed&nbsp;
teachers, they interviewed the references that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.480
put down. They talked to people that I didn't put&nbsp;
down. They want to know about your life. They want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:31.200
to know who you are as a person, especially for&nbsp;
students and for people like me who have a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:37.680
more of a, I would say, troubled background. I'm&nbsp;
not a straight-A student. I didn't go to the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:45.960
schools in life. I had a clean record, but if&nbsp;
you look at this sort of supporting evidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.100
it's like, well, this person's been in&nbsp;
trouble over the course of their life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:56.580
and they talked to my professors, and one&nbsp;
of my professors hated me. And so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:02.160
conversation was like, it was just so weird.
We had this, I don't know if other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.960
had this, but in physics, first-year physics,&nbsp;
they had this computer program that generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.100
your assignments for you. So everybody&nbsp;
in the class literally had this different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:16.800
assignment and each question was different.&nbsp;
There was 10 versions of question one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:23.280
and then all the numbers were different. So even&nbsp;
if you had the same version of question one, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.720
you could find somebody else with it, you had the&nbsp;
same version, you would have different numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:33.120
so you couldn't just copy the answer. So all&nbsp;
the kids in school, all the rich kids basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:40.680
bought the same tutor, and this tutor for 20&nbsp;
bucks or whatever, you gave him your sheet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.940
he spit out the answers for you because he had&nbsp;
all the 10 versions of the question. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:49.680
just plug in your numbers, and you'd go to school.
And the way to submit your assignment wasn't you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.680
actually submitted paper. This is the dawn&nbsp;
of the internet, like 1998-ish. And so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.480
would submit your assignment almost like over&nbsp;
Telnet. You would log in and it would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.980
"What's your answer to question one?"&nbsp;
And you would submit your answer. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:11.340
I was like, this takes a lot of time. I've got&nbsp;
to work full-time. I don't know if I can spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:15.840
the 20 hours a week it actually takes to do this.&nbsp;
But I was in computer science, so it was like I'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:23.100
write a little program that just sort of goes from&nbsp;
-1000 to 1000 and guesses in increments of 0.1.&nbsp;

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:29.040
And so I did this little program and it worked&nbsp;
great. I got 20 out of 20 on every assignment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:35.040
until March of my first year. The teacher comes&nbsp;
in and he used one of those transparencies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.847
and I don't know if people remember&nbsp;
transparencies, but basically —&nbsp;

00:08:37.847 --> 00:08:41.160
Tim Ferriss: The overhead projectors.
Shane Parrish: So it's like a PowerPoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:46.320
graph that he puts on this projector, and it's&nbsp;
like average guesses on the y-axis and student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.540
number on the x-axis. And the average number&nbsp;
of guesses per question was, I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:59.940
17 or something. And mine was in the millions.
So he's like, "I want to see this student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.080
after class." And I was careful. I had&nbsp;
read all the policies and procedures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.140
and it says, "Unlimited guessing."
Tim Ferriss: So you're submitting like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.560
million guesses via Telnet, is that right?
Shane Parrish: Yeah, I'm not doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.900
anything. I just basically —
Tim Ferriss: So there's no downside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.525
for number of guesses. You're only rewarded for&nbsp;
getting the answer right in one of those guesses.&nbsp;

00:09:18.525 --> 00:09:20.880
Shane Parrish: Exactly.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, so why not? Yeah.&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.100
Shane Parrish: I read all the policies and&nbsp;
procedures. I was very careful about reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.960
all the rules to make sure I wasn't violating&nbsp;
any rules. But I would go to bed, and I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:34.680
just basically hit run on this program,&nbsp;
and I would wake up, and I would have my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:41.700
full assignments submitted, 20 out of 20.
Tim Ferriss: So Shane gets retained after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:46.440
class. Was it to discipline you and wag&nbsp;
a finger at you, or was there something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.100
else that came with the conversation?
Shane Parrish: Oh, so he was like, "You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:54.000
going to get zero on all your assignments."&nbsp;
And I was like, "What? That's not fair at all."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.660
And he's like, "Well, we're sort of retroactively&nbsp;
changing the rules." And I was like, "That doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:06.420
make any sense. That's not fair. All the&nbsp;
rich kids are hiring tutors. I can't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.620
that. It doesn't make sense. Just go with&nbsp;
my exam mark as my mark," sort of thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:15.600
And he said "No." And basically, the&nbsp;
assignments were so heavily weighted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:23.280
that I ended up failing physics because of this.&nbsp;
It was so crazy. And so when I'm getting my job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.480
they actually talked to this teacher.
Tim Ferriss: Now how did you come in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.400
touch with — I understand why he would have an ax&nbsp;
to grind or at least it sounds like the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.940
guy who would have maybe something scornful and&nbsp;
spiteful to say. How did you even get connected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:45.720
with an intelligence agency? Were they recruiting&nbsp;
in certain centers nearby? Did they canvas the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.200
whole country? I don't know how it works.
Shane Parrish: It was just sort of luck. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:55.500
was doing computer science at a time when the rise&nbsp;
of the internet. I didn't want to work for Nortel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:00.720
or any of these big companies. I really wanted to&nbsp;
work for an intelligence agency. I thought that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:07.920
would be really fun and coincidence that they&nbsp;
happen to be at my school. I like problems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:14.880
are not really solvable, that are intractable,&nbsp;
that are big and gnarly and that you can never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:21.720
quite solve, but you can get better at. My parents&nbsp;
were in the military, so I had a patriotic sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:28.440
of giving back to my country. How fortunate&nbsp;
am I to be born into a country with freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:34.380
and good school system and a good healthcare&nbsp;
system? And I was taught and brought up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.520
way that you have an obligation to give back.
And for anybody that knows me, I'm not going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.660
to give back standing on the front lines with a&nbsp;
machine gun. That's just dangerous for everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:49.320
involved. But if I could use my brain in a&nbsp;
way that was giving back to the people and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:54.600
the community and the country in which I grew up,&nbsp;
I thought that that was a really good win-win,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:59.520
interesting problems, giving back at the same&nbsp;
time. They came to the school, I applied for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.680
a job, and if I didn't get it, I was basically&nbsp;
going to go back and do my MBA right away and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:10.020
something else. But I ended up getting it, ended&nbsp;
up working with an incredible group of talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:16.080
people starting what then went on to become&nbsp;
the largest part of the intelligence agency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.420
I think now, or the most valuable part of it.
Tim Ferriss: And for people in the US what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:27.180
that agency be comparable to, if you can say?&nbsp;
Would it be comparable to the NSA, comparable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:32.501
CIA, comparable to other three-letter acronym?
Shane Parrish: Yeah, that would be the NSA.&nbsp;

00:12:32.501 --> 00:12:37.800
Tim Ferriss: Okay, got it. And I am going&nbsp;
to take a hard left here to ask you about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:43.560
maybe since you like intractable problems,&nbsp;
to ask you about one that I know a lot of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:49.500
friends are contending with, which is thinking&nbsp;
about how moving around helps or handicaps their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:57.840
kids. You moved around a ton. You have kids.&nbsp;
How do you think that experience of moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:02.520
from place to place, school to school,&nbsp;
affected you positively and negatively,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:10.380
or in the pro and con columns, so to speak?&nbsp;
And how do you think about that or how would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:16.200
you suggest other people think about that if&nbsp;
they currently have very mobile lives and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:20.220
wondering how that will affect their kids?
Shane Parrish: There's a couple different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:24.420
ways to think about that as it&nbsp;
relates to my experience, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:31.680
I knew I was moving, so I knew at the end of the&nbsp;
year I was going to be in a different school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:40.680
That became apparent at least by grade six or&nbsp;
seven. And so every year I could experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:45.180
with myself and not consciously, but&nbsp;
I could emphasize different parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:50.880
myself. Do I fit in with the jocks? Do I&nbsp;
fit in with the nerds? What is my group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:59.160
people? And I could play a role for a year.
The downside is I knew that I was moving in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:05.400
a year, and so I knew all of these relationships&nbsp;
were sort of transitory. So I think the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:15.840
impact it had on me is, even in adulthood, it took&nbsp;
me a long time to sort of develop "This person's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:21.180
going to be in my life for a long period of time.&nbsp;
This isn't like a transitory relationship," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:27.000
transitory means it is just going to go. And so&nbsp;
you don't invest in it. You don't invest in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.740
the same way that you would invest in your best&nbsp;
friend or somebody like that because you knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:36.360
the closer you got to that person at the end&nbsp;
of the year, the harder it would be to leave.&nbsp;

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:41.640
And so at that point in time, it was&nbsp;
really difficult for me coming out of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:48.540
becoming a university student, and then joining&nbsp;
the workforce, to not have this mental default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.540 --> 00:14:54.240
of this is going to go away or I might not see&nbsp;
this person? But on the flipside, it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:58.920
I'm going to work with this person for the next&nbsp;
15 years. And I think that was really hard for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:04.620
to sort of catch on to. It took a few years for me&nbsp;
to really start investing in those friendships and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:10.740
those relationships in the way that I wanted to.
Tim Ferriss: If you were sitting at a dinner table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:16.680
with some, let's say they're relatively new&nbsp;
parents, they have kids under the age of two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:23.220
so the kids aren't going to school for a little&nbsp;
bit, but it's not the distant future. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:29.400
have a life where they spend X number of months&nbsp;
in one place and Y number of months in another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.640
and maybe they're thinking about living in&nbsp;
a different state or a different country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:40.380
but they don't want to screw their kids up&nbsp;
in maybe indulging those explorations. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:44.460
the same time, they're not sure if they&nbsp;
want to be in one place in a fixed way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.260
What would you say to those people?
Shane Parrish: My advice, for what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.340
worth, is I don't think it really matters much&nbsp;
until they're sort of grade seven and eight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:58.200
and certainly in high school. It matters a lot in&nbsp;
high school, the consistency, the friend group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:06.120
the sort of knowing what to expect. Because&nbsp;
if I map myself to just my kids and their age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.900
there's a lot of uncertainty in their life.&nbsp;
There's a lot of things going on that we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:14.280
see. And then adding on to that sort of like&nbsp;
you're going to be in a different school with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:21.240
different system with the different teachers, you&nbsp;
can't really thrive in that environment easily.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.800
That doesn't mean you can't thrive. It just&nbsp;
means you're making it harder than it has to be.&nbsp;

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:32.340
But before that, I would say up until grade six,&nbsp;
again, just going on my experience with my kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:38.640
what they're really learning in school and from&nbsp;
friendships is how do we be social? How do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:43.440
interact with other people? How do we get in&nbsp;
the habit of going to school? How do we learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.660
to read and learn to do basic math? And that you&nbsp;
can sort of get anywhere in the world really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:53.460
really easily. Grade seven, at least for&nbsp;
my kids, is when it started to change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:59.700
and that's in part because of the schools that I&nbsp;
sent them to. I think it starts to matter in grade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:02.580
seven increasingly and definitely in high school.
Tim Ferriss: What type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:06.720
schools did you send them to?
Shane Parrish: I'm a big believer that kids should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:14.760
be challenged. And I don't think that the schools&nbsp;
around my house challenged the kids. So I found a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:20.280
school in Ottawa that I had to drive to and drop&nbsp;
off my kids. And I'm the type of parent that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:27.480
like, "You can do things you don't believe you're&nbsp;
capable of." And so starting in grade seven, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.740
kids were getting 90 minutes of homework a night,&nbsp;
they were taking school in a different language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:38.340
and they were pushed and challenged. And the&nbsp;
standards of this school, and I take a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:43.380
flack for this even from friends of mine, but&nbsp;
the standards of this school are unrelenting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:50.340
They don't care how you feel. And that is&nbsp;
very counter-cultural in today's message.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:55.080
But if you go in there and you haven't done your&nbsp;
homework, it does not matter. You get a zero.&nbsp;

00:17:55.080 --> 00:18:00.600
And to put things in context, we went to my&nbsp;
grandmother's funeral in May, and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:05.880
one day late getting their essay in, and they&nbsp;
were one day late, and they got 10 percent off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:12.120
their essay because they were one day late. And&nbsp;
the school's approach to this is like, look, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:17.220
want to produce effective adults, and that means&nbsp;
you're going to fail in grade seven. You're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:21.600
going to fail academically because everybody there&nbsp;
is really smart. You're going to fail at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.160
little life things that you should have taken&nbsp;
that into account when you planned your essay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:32.280
submission. And they put it on the students, and&nbsp;
they do it in such a way that the kids, at first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:41.580
my son was crying every night because he is&nbsp;
naturally really, really smart. And he goes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:45.947
the school, and it's in a different language, and&nbsp;
now it's 90 minutes of work a night and they're —&nbsp;

00:18:45.947 --> 00:18:50.460
Tim Ferriss: Is it French? What is the language?
Shane Parrish: Yeah, so it's French. And so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:54.660
don't speak French. I didn't speak French to him&nbsp;
at home. He hasn't spoken French in two and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:59.340
half years because of COVID basically. And all&nbsp;
of a sudden, he's thrust into a French school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:07.440
and they're unrelenting. So he was so naturally&nbsp;
smart that he didn't have to work for anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:13.860
and that school eliminates that. They're like,&nbsp;
"No, you have to work and if you don't work, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:19.740
can't get good grades." And it was so hard on him,&nbsp;
and I'm the parent who's like, "Hey, I love you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:25.380
I know this is really hard. I know you can do it.&nbsp;
Let's shift our mindset around this." And by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:32.340
end of, I would say, December, he was loving it.&nbsp;
He loved being challenged. And when it came time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:37.440
to pick a high school, he literally, his filter&nbsp;
was what school is going to challenge me the most?&nbsp;

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:42.960
And I was like, "Oh, that school did an awesome&nbsp;
job." I'm so happy that that's the way he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:47.940
thinking about this. It's not about grades. I&nbsp;
would rather him basically fail at doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:55.800
hard than really succeed at doing something easy&nbsp;
because I feel like we get wrapped up as parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.460 --> 00:20:02.340
our kids' success is our success, and so&nbsp;
their grades become our success. But really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.620
what we want to do is produce independent adults&nbsp;
who are capable of handling the ups and downs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:11.520
life. And that means that they're going to fail,&nbsp;
they're going to struggle, they're going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:16.320
teachers they don't like, they're going to get&nbsp;
grades that maybe they do or don't deserve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:22.980
And so I think about all of these things.
Tim Ferriss: I would say an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:33.660
a similar experience in the sense that I&nbsp;
went to a very easy, reasonably disorganized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:39.180
set of schools up until the end of my&nbsp;
freshman year in high school and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:46.380
was transferred, and this was my idea, but I&nbsp;
transferred to a very difficult school, for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:53.520
at least, in New Hampshire, boarding school. And I&nbsp;
got absolutely crushed, schooling six days a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.520 --> 00:21:01.980
mandatory sports every semester. You had classes,&nbsp;
also, sixth period, I think it was, after sports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:08.400
and then you would have seated meals with coat&nbsp;
and tie and so on several times a week and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:16.800
whole nine, and was so challenging. I went from&nbsp;
being easily top of the pack to maybe bottom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:24.060
the middle of the pack, maybe lower, right? I was&nbsp;
suddenly undistinguished from any intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:30.420
perspective. And kids who were at this school,&nbsp;
St. Paul's, knew how to work, too, a lot of them.&nbsp;

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:40.080
And I'm so grateful for it because they were&nbsp;
incredibly tough, discipline was demanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:47.640
and everything after that was relatively&nbsp;
easy, even Princeton and so on, the workload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:53.460
no problem. And I think in retrospect that it&nbsp;
was really critical to my formation. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:57.060
also where I was introduced to Japanese and&nbsp;
other languages and had my exchange experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:04.140
which changed everything for me. But being taught&nbsp;
early that the world will not conform to all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:10.320
your unique needs and preferences is, like you&nbsp;
said, it's very counter-cultural. I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:17.940
if you train a child to expect, this is true,&nbsp;
train anyone to expect they are going to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:22.200
decisions, what they want is always going&nbsp;
to be taken into account, what they like is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:28.380
always going to be at the top of other priority&nbsp;
lists, it's setting them up for a lifetime of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:36.240
disappointment and disorder and depression&nbsp;
probably, all at once. Anyway, that's maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:40.260
a strong response, but I agree with the approach.
Shane Parrish: Pause for one sec. I want to know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:46.080
you said this was your decision. What made&nbsp;
you decide that? Where did that come from?&nbsp;

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:51.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I guess it was informed&nbsp;
by a couple of other inputs. So one was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.360 --> 00:22:59.280
I was very lucky. Luck plays into all of this so&nbsp;
much. One friend of mine had escaped Long Island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:05.040
and he had gone to St. Paul's, and he came back,&nbsp;
and he basically said, "You've got to get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:12.480
here. You have to escape." And it happened that&nbsp;
around the same time, I think it was a math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:17.760
teacher and one other teacher effectively said&nbsp;
the same thing. They're like, "Tim, I can't go any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:24.900
faster, but you're not going to get what you need&nbsp;
here." And there wasn't really a strong suggestion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:32.040
as to a solution in that case. But those things&nbsp;
all combined to piquing my curiosity about these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:36.000
things called boarding school. I didn't know&nbsp;
what boarding schools were, outside of a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:41.940
stories from my friend's experience. So I went&nbsp;
to the local library back when you had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.680
this and took out these really thick guides to&nbsp;
boarding schools, tried to figure things out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:53.280
and ultimately got a few scholarships. And&nbsp;
then my whole extended family helped pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:57.720
for this thing because it's expensive. And my&nbsp;
immediate family didn't have very much money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.720 --> 00:24:03.480
So I was very lucky that my grandparents and&nbsp;
other people also chipped in to make it happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:13.080
And I think that fundamentally, I had also been&nbsp;
competing in sports. And I recognized in sports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:18.300
if you're not losing routinely, you're&nbsp;
not training with the right people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:23.460
Does that make sense? At least in wrestling. It's&nbsp;
like if you were to train on a tennis team and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:30.720
your goal was to become the best tennis player&nbsp;
possible, if you are just annihilating the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:36.120
you're kind of on the wrong team if your goal&nbsp;
is to compete in larger and larger arenas. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:44.520
I appreciated that and enjoyed that with respect&nbsp;
to competition. I was very competitive. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:50.940
couldn't really apply that to the academics in any&nbsp;
meaningful way until I went to St. Paul's and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:57.120
I got my ass kicked. But it was the beneficial&nbsp;
adaptive ass-kicking that I think I really needed.&nbsp;

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:02.880
Shane Parrish: I think we miss&nbsp;
that, right? School has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:09.300
almost too easy. We're not challenging the kids&nbsp;
and we're not holding them to a high enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:15.540
standard, and it's causing all these downstream&nbsp;
problems later on in life. At least that's my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:20.100
view. And I want my kids to be challenged. I want&nbsp;
them to fail. I want them to learn how to overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:24.900
these obstacles. And you must've gone through&nbsp;
something similar when you're going from sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:32.400
of lead dog to maybe bottom, middle of the pack.&nbsp;
"Where's my identity?" You're not just dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.400 --> 00:25:37.500
with, "How do I develop the systems to study and&nbsp;
get the work done? And all these people already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:42.240
know it, so I'm behind." But you're dealing&nbsp;
with all these identity crises in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:48.240
your ego and how you think about yourself.
Tim Ferriss: Oh, totally. For the first six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:55.320
months, I felt like I was always behind. I was&nbsp;
always behind. I was always having to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.320 --> 00:26:00.420
what I wouldn't finish because I just was not&nbsp;
fast enough. I was not organized enough. I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:06.840
not have the time management skills and figuring&nbsp;
out when I could take a 20-minute nap so that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:13.680
could then stay up an extra hour and a half&nbsp;
after dinner to finish A, B, and C, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:19.500
manage to do this other thing in preparation so&nbsp;
that I'd be well-prepared for athletic practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:24.120
the next day after a full day of coursework,&nbsp;
after chapel every morning just about. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.860
it was six days a week we had chapel, either&nbsp;
five or six days, which is mostly roll call of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:31.800
sorts and announcements. But there were also&nbsp;
hymns and this, that, and the other thing.&nbsp;

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:37.920
It was a very, very full schedule. And I'm&nbsp;
grateful for that. And people listening have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.160
probably heard the expression, "If you want&nbsp;
something done, give it to a busy man." It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:46.440
could be woman, of course. But the idea being if&nbsp;
someone is busy and they have a full schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:51.000
they learn how to juggle that full schedule.&nbsp;
And if you have an incomplete schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:56.760
I think especially in those formative years in&nbsp;
high school, you do not by necessity develop the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.760 --> 00:27:01.860
time management skills that if you really want to&nbsp;
charge hard later in life, you are going to need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:08.100
And I feel lucky, very, very lucky. If I hadn't&nbsp;
had that friend who was the proof of concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:17.280
who knows? Who knows where I would be?
So let's come back to your life story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:24.900
What was 68131, I think it was .blogger.com&nbsp;
if I'm getting that right. What was that?&nbsp;

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:29.940
Shane Parrish: So when I was working at the&nbsp;
intelligence agency, I started two weeks before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:38.220
September 11th. Literally, two weeks later,&nbsp;
the world changed and everybody was thrust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:44.940
into roles and responsibilities over the coming&nbsp;
decade, basically, that they weren't ready for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:49.080
nobody could have prepared you&nbsp;
for, you just had to do your best.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:57.960
And I remember being put in charge of something&nbsp;
that I didn't feel like I could quite handle. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:04.260
I'm making decisions that sort of impact&nbsp;
myself and impact my team and impact my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:12.720
country and impact troops in theater. And&nbsp;
I have no idea what I'm doing. And so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:18.840
went to my boss one day after a particularly&nbsp;
challenging operation. I said, "Here's how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:23.220
I thought about it. I don't think I'm ready&nbsp;
for this job. I don't think I can do this."&nbsp;

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:25.980
And he basically just looked at me and&nbsp;
he was like, "You're all we've got.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:31.560
What else are we going to do? We're all just doing&nbsp;
the best we can. Get back to work." And I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:38.040
like, "That's the worst pep talk I have ever heard&nbsp;
in my life." And so I was like, "I think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:44.340
deserve better than me. And what can I do if I&nbsp;
can't sort get out of this role?" So I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:47.700
following people around in the organization. I&nbsp;
was like, "How do these people make decisions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:54.420
What can I learn from them?" And I went back and&nbsp;
did an MBA. And when I was doing my MBA, I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.420 --> 00:29:00.720
rediscovered Charlie Munger and Warren Buffett.&nbsp;
And part of my MBA, it was so useless. And I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:06.240
mean that in a negative way, it just wasn't giving&nbsp;
me what I needed at that particular moment to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:11.580
better at the things that I wanted to get better&nbsp;
at. But Charlie Munger and Warren Buffett were.&nbsp;

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:18.960
And so I created this website, which was&nbsp;
68131-1440.blogger.com, which I don't own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:22.920
today. Somebody else has, like, taken this&nbsp;
over, probably because I talk about it a lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:29.280
But the website was basically the ZIP code for&nbsp;
Berkshire Hathaway, dash, the unit number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:36.120
Berkshire Hathaway in Kiewit Plaza. And it was my&nbsp;
online journal. Now keep in mind, this is like — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:39.720
don't know what year it is, but I'm not allowed to&nbsp;
have a LinkedIn profile. I'm not allowed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:46.380
any social — you Google me, I do not exist. The&nbsp;
intelligence agency would be livid if you had any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:52.020
sort of public profile, any sort of anything at&nbsp;
all. And here I am, I'm like, "Oh, I'm just going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:57.600
to create this online journal and this is how I'm&nbsp;
going to chronicle my learning and development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:02.580
I'm just going to take notes and I'm going to&nbsp;
connect these ideas and I'm going to synthesize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.580 --> 00:30:08.280
them and I'm going to compress them. And that is&nbsp;
the act of reflection. So I'm going to basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:12.180
learn things on my own. I'm going to reflect on&nbsp;
them, and then I'm going to write about them."&nbsp;

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:17.640
And so I started this website. And then&nbsp;
eventually, I don't even know how at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.780 --> 00:30:24.660
we got to, I think it was like 25,000 readers&nbsp;
or something. And then all of a sudden I started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:30.240
getting these emails, which is like, "I hate&nbsp;
typing in the name of your website. Can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:36.120
basically change the name?" And I was like, "Fine.&nbsp;
I'll just call it Farnam Street," which was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:40.980
homage to Buffett and Munger, who I know have&nbsp;
played such a critical role in your life too.&nbsp;

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:47.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, they have. I remember somebody,&nbsp;
I can't recall who it was, but dug up the video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:58.320
of my wavering voice at the Berkshire Hathaway&nbsp;
shareholder meeting when I asked my question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:05.580
from the nosebleed seats. I have been informed&nbsp;
by them for sure. I have incredible respect for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:09.840
both of them. Were you introduced to them by&nbsp;
Buffett: The Making of an American Capitalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:14.400
or how did you get introduced to them?
Shane Parrish: Yeah, so I read Buffett:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:19.200
The Making of an American Capitalist, I think in&nbsp;
university. But everything got put on the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.200 --> 00:31:24.780
burner once I joined the intelligence agency&nbsp;
and the world changed. September 11th changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:31.320
everything. I didn't really come up. I worked&nbsp;
six days a week, 12 hours a day for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:35.460
didn't really have a lot of vacations,&nbsp;
didn't have a lot of long weekends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.460 --> 00:31:40.860
and everybody else was doing the exact same thing.&nbsp;
And it was the best time of my life if that makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:46.080
sense too. Not only you're working incredibly&nbsp;
hard, you're working on amazing problems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:50.820
you're really attracted to, and you're working&nbsp;
with a group of highly intelligent, dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:55.320
people. It doesn't really get much better than&nbsp;
that from a quality-of-life point of view.&nbsp;

00:31:55.320 --> 00:32:02.580
But everything, all these outside interests&nbsp;
got put on hold. I'm coming home, I'm spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.580 --> 00:32:08.100
a little bit of time with the woman who was my&nbsp;
girlfriend at the time. And then I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:13.560
bed and I'm waking up and just repeating on rinse.&nbsp;
And then the MBA was sort of a way for me to step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:18.540
back from that for a couple years, do a less&nbsp;
intensive role there while doing a full-time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:23.400
MBA. And that was when I got reconnected. I was&nbsp;
like, "Oh, yeah, this Warren Buffett guy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:27.420
Charlie Munger. But what are they doing? They're&nbsp;
making decisions in the real world and they seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:35.520
really good at it. So what can I learn from that?"
Tim Ferriss: When you had the somewhat awkward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:42.780
numerical string, right, when you had the&nbsp;
ZIP code as the URL, if you remember, were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:48.000
there any particular pieces that struck a chord&nbsp;
with folks? Those are nontrivial numbers you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:56.400
talking about in terms of readership. Were there&nbsp;
any particular threads or pieces that come to mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.400 --> 00:33:00.660
that really struck a chord in the early days?
Shane Parrish: I'd have to go back to be sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:08.040
but I think there's stuff about — I was trying&nbsp;
to figure out how Daniel Kahneman came up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:15.060
cognitive biases. And Warren Buffett and Charlie&nbsp;
Munger didn't really talk about cognitive biases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:21.480
but seemed to avoid them. And so I spent&nbsp;
a lot of time trying to reconcile how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:29.220
cognitive biases can help you in the real world.
And so what I sort of hit on, at least from my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:34.560
point of view, is cognitive biases are really good&nbsp;
at explaining why you made a mistake in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.560 --> 00:33:39.300
but they're really poor at preventing you&nbsp;
from making mistakes in the future. So they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:45.120
intellectually very satisfying, but they don't&nbsp;
really prevent you — if you create a checklist of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:50.820
cognitive biases and you're a smart person, you're&nbsp;
just basically going to outsmart yourself. "Am I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:55.620
overconfident? No, I'm not overconfident,"&nbsp;
right? "I've got this, I did the work." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.620 --> 00:34:00.120
so the story you tell yourself, you sort of&nbsp;
talk yourself out of them in a lot of ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.740
which is a bit of an irony to them.
Tim Ferriss: Now tell me more about that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:08.880
What do you mean by talking yourself out of them?
Shane Parrish: Well, you come up with a checklist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:13.140
A lot of people have tried this. They come up with&nbsp;
a checklist of cognitive biases and they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:13.140 --> 00:34:17.160
"Okay, I'm going to see if this applies to me."
Tim Ferriss: So sunk cost fallacy, this, that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:19.800
and the other thing, expectancy&nbsp;
bias, or whatever it might be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:26.280
Just putting together a list of&nbsp;
things that one is intending to avoid.&nbsp;

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:33.900
Shane Parrish: Yes, yeah. But considers sunk&nbsp;
cost. So you make a decision and then you pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.900 --> 00:34:41.400
out your checklist and you're like, "Am I&nbsp;
considering sunk cost or am I just making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:47.220
this fresh?" And you've already decided at&nbsp;
this moment. So you're just justifying and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.220 --> 00:34:49.200
rationalizing the decision you already have.
Tim Ferriss: I see what you're saying.&nbsp;

00:34:49.200 --> 00:34:54.420
Shane Parrish: You tell yourself a story&nbsp;
about why sunk cost doesn't apply in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:57.887
situation or why you're not overconfident&nbsp;
in this situation. And the smarter you are —&nbsp;

00:34:57.887 --> 00:35:01.005
Tim Ferriss: Use confirmation&nbsp;
bias to address your other biases.&nbsp;

00:35:01.005 --> 00:35:04.860
Shane Parrish: Exactly, right? And&nbsp;
the smarter you are, the better those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.860 --> 00:35:08.820
stories get and the more believable they get.
Tim Ferriss: Actually, I'd love to get your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:16.260
help defining a phrase, just I suppose explaining&nbsp;
a phrase and then segueing into a conversation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:23.820
around mental models and cognitive biases and so&nbsp;
on. So ordinary moments determine your position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:29.640
and your position determines your&nbsp;
results. So what is meant by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:34.320
line and what is meant by position?
Shane Parrish: So the counterintuitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:40.200
insight that I had about decision-making&nbsp;
from studying sort of Buffett and Munger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.200 --> 00:35:45.840
Peter Kaufman and a host of other people who've&nbsp;
influenced my thinking is that they almost never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:52.380
find themselves out of position. And if you want&nbsp;
to think of it as position as your circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:56.760
they're never forced by circumstances into&nbsp;
doing something that they don't want to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.760 --> 00:36:00.960
And often that means they look like an idiot&nbsp;
in the short term, but in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:06.420
they win. If you consider Buffett, for example,&nbsp;
we've talked about he closes down his partnership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.140 --> 00:36:13.800
during a bull market in the '60s because he&nbsp;
doesn't know how to invest anymore. He's lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:19.560
touch. He can't find what worked for him. He&nbsp;
reopens it later in terms of Berkshire Hathaway.&nbsp;

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:26.160
Time magazine, I think it was 1999, he's&nbsp;
on the cover as being out of touch. 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:32.400
stock market burst. Right now, as of today, I&nbsp;
think as of last quarter, he's got 150 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:37.680
dollars on the balance sheet. And so what he's&nbsp;
always doing is everybody thinks he's out of touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:43.020
and he looks like an idiot. But he always wins&nbsp;
because no matter what the outcome is, he wins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:48.960
If the stock market goes up, he wins. If the stock&nbsp;
market crashes, he wins because he's put himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.960 --> 00:36:54.540
in a position where no matter what happens, he can&nbsp;
take advantage of circumstances rather than having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.540 --> 00:37:00.120
circumstances take advantage of him. And I think&nbsp;
that that's a key element we don't realize. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:07.740
put Warren Buffett in a bad position where all of&nbsp;
his options are bad, it doesn't matter how smart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:12.240
he is, it doesn't matter how Warren Buffett he is.
Everybody looks like an idiot when they're in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:15.587
bad position and everybody looks like a&nbsp;
genius when they're in a good position.&nbsp;

00:37:15.587 --> 00:37:20.220
Tim Ferriss: Do you then create that position by&nbsp;
having a handful of commandments? And I'm making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:24.240
these up. I'm not saying this is what Buffett&nbsp;
does because I haven't tracked him as closely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:29.940
as you have. But for instance, I know someone&nbsp;
who's cut from a similar cloth. And he's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:29.940 --> 00:37:36.360
"No short positions, no leverage." And he has&nbsp;
four or five rules that he follows, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:43.920
he doesn't always beat the S&amp;P 500, let's say,&nbsp;
but he's very rarely in a position where he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:52.560
a distressed seller or is forced into a bad&nbsp;
position. So he's able to play a very long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:59.700
game. So I guess please keep going. But&nbsp;
I'm wondering how you consistently create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:04.200
that type of position, if that makes sense.
Shane Parrish: So let me talk about it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:08.760
terms of finance and then apply it to something&nbsp;
else that's totally — you would think it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.760 --> 00:38:13.260
apply to. So in finance, you can think of&nbsp;
low debt, you can think of saving money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:18.600
you can think of always having dry powder. It&nbsp;
doesn't matter if you have the best idea in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:22.680
world. If you can't take advantage of that idea,&nbsp;
you might as well not have the idea. You have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:22.680 --> 00:38:27.300
be in a position to take advantage of the ideas&nbsp;
that you have. And you have to be in a position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:32.760
where somebody can't call you in at the exact&nbsp;
wrong moment because that's really expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:37.740
as everybody sort of learned during the&nbsp;
great financial crisis and has now forgotten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:38.700 --> 00:38:43.500
We seem to relearn these lessons over and&nbsp;
over again. But positioning applies to so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:47.760
many other things that we don't think about.
And you can think about it basically as in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:54.660
your sleep. Did you work out? Are you healthy?&nbsp;
Are you eating well? Are you investing in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:59.940
relationship with your partner? What position am I&nbsp;
in the moment that I get into a fight or argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:06.240
or disagreement with my partner or my spouse? And&nbsp;
we don't think about the position we're in at that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:10.440
moment. But if you imagine that there's a patch&nbsp;
of grass between you and them, is that grass dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:15.300
or is it wet? Have I watered that for months, in&nbsp;
which case the spark isn't going to light it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:22.680
fire, or is the smallest little spark going to&nbsp;
start this forest fire? And the position that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:28.560
we bring into those arguments matters a lot in&nbsp;
terms of the quality of the outcomes that we get.&nbsp;

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:33.420
And I'll give you another example. We were&nbsp;
talking about kids earlier, and I assume a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:38.400
lot of people listening have kids. One of my&nbsp;
kids came home with an exam and he got a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:44.160
terrible mark. And he hands it to me. And he's&nbsp;
like a teenager, so he hands it to me, he's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:49.800
"I did the best I could," walks away. And normally&nbsp;
the conversation ends there. And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:53.940
"Okay. Now is not the moment that we're going&nbsp;
to talk about this because I know we've got this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:58.140
teenage attitude thing going on, but we're going&nbsp;
to circle back to this." And so later that night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:58.140 --> 00:40:03.660
I was like, "Talk to me about what it means to&nbsp;
do the best you can." And what he told me about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:09.720
the best he could was when he sat down to write&nbsp;
that test from 10:00 to 11:00, he focused all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:15.300
his energy and all of his effort on it. He made&nbsp;
the best decisions possible from 10:00 to 11:00.&nbsp;

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:22.320
But what he forgot or didn't really appreciate is&nbsp;
what position was he in at 10:00 a.m. when he sat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:27.300
down to write that test? Did he fight with his&nbsp;
brother in the morning? Yep, he did. Did he eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.300 --> 00:40:32.040
a healthy breakfast? Nope, he didn't. Did he get&nbsp;
a good night's sleep? Nope. He was up late. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:38.460
he study? No, he sort of half-assed it. And so&nbsp;
what happened was he was in a bad position at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:45.120
the moment he sat down to write that test. He was&nbsp;
ill-prepared to write the test, but yes, he did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:49.980
the best he could in that moment. And so often&nbsp;
we think about life in terms of, "How do I do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.980 --> 00:40:54.720
the best I can in this particular moment?" rather&nbsp;
than, "How do I put myself in the best position?"&nbsp;

00:40:54.720 --> 00:41:00.660
And part of that is we're always predicting, we're&nbsp;
predicting creatures. And so we're always trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:05.400
to gauge what the outcome is and maximize towards&nbsp;
that outcome. "I want to go all in on Tesla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:10.980
I want to go all in on whatever I want to maximize&nbsp;
my return." And what we don't think about is like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:15.060
"Okay. Well, what is the cost of doing that?&nbsp;
And what if I'm wrong? And how do I position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.060 --> 00:41:20.640
myself for multiple possible futures where,&nbsp;
what if my idea is not correct? Can I still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:26.220
survive and can I thrive on that?" And if you&nbsp;
look back and you sort of look back at Carnegie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:32.520
and Rockefeller and all these historical&nbsp;
people who built these great businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:36.780
including Buffett, they always take&nbsp;
advantage of a weakness in somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:41.460
else's position when their position is strong.
Tim Ferriss: Who else would you, and I know you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:47.940
mentioned a few names, but outside of Buffett&nbsp;
and Munger, who else would you put on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.380 --> 00:41:54.660
shortlist? And not to make you pick favorites,&nbsp;
I'm sure you give a very long list. But just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:54.660 --> 00:42:01.440
off the cuff, if you had to pick five to&nbsp;
10 people let's just say, could be four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:09.480
but let's aim for five or slightly more people&nbsp;
who you really respect as incisive, deliberate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.480 --> 00:42:14.760
decision-makers. Who would make that list?
Shane Parrish: So we have a mutual friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:21.600
Naval Ravikant. He could be up there. Patrick&nbsp;
Collison would sort of be up there. Kat Cole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.600 --> 00:42:23.985
who works at Athletic Greens.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.&nbsp;

00:42:23.985 --> 00:42:29.580
Shane Parrish: One of my favorite people.&nbsp;
She's always one step ahead. She's amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:34.860
I think that there's very few people&nbsp;
who are well-positioned in everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:41.160
If you look at somebody who's extremely good&nbsp;
at one thing like Buffett, that comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:48.060
extreme weaknesses. But you can have a list&nbsp;
of exemplars in your head almost of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:53.160
people who are good at different positions. But&nbsp;
you have to pick what direction you're going.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:54.120 --> 00:43:00.780
And then what positions do you need to be good&nbsp;
at to be good in that direction? And so for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:00.780 --> 00:43:06.660
I circle back to core things which people don't&nbsp;
really think about, but they're core to everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:12.540
we do in terms of health and relationships&nbsp;
and friendship and purpose and community. Am I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.540 --> 00:43:17.820
working on something that I want to be working&nbsp;
on? Am I moving closer to my destination and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:22.560
is my destination the place I want to go?
And I think those questions are stuff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:28.020
we don't really ask ourselves. We're always so&nbsp;
focused on, "How do we accomplish the outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:33.360
we want?" rather than, "What are the key elements&nbsp;
to this process that allow me to play for a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:38.700
time? Am I going in the right direction&nbsp;
and do I want to get to where I'm going?"&nbsp;

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:47.280
Tim Ferriss: So I am going to come back to&nbsp;
just the broad question of how to incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:48.660 --> 00:43:52.980
thinking about cognitive biases if I'm&nbsp;
saying that correctly and/or mental.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:54.360 --> 00:43:57.780
I guess those are different. I'd love to&nbsp;
have you — let's begin there. How would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:03.360
distinguish or define mental models? And then&nbsp;
I'm going to go micro and then we'll zoom out.&nbsp;

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:08.580
Shane Parrish: So a great way to think about&nbsp;
mental models is the source of all bad decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.580 --> 00:44:13.980
is blind spots. Mental models allow you to see&nbsp;
a problem through a different lens. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:21.360
adopt a different persona. A great example of&nbsp;
a mental model is like, "How would Tim Ferriss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:21.360 --> 00:44:27.960
think about this?" And then I adapt what I know&nbsp;
about you and I try to see the problem from your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:33.660
perspective. And if I can see the problem from&nbsp;
your perspective, now I've done the one thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:39.360
actually gets me out of cognitive biases, which is&nbsp;
the source of all cognitive biases are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:46.440
we believe that what we see is all there is.
And we learned about this sort of in physics in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:51.300
grade nine I think where you're standing on this&nbsp;
train and you're carrying this ball. And the train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.300 --> 00:44:55.860
is moving at 60 miles an hour, and how fast is the&nbsp;
ball moving? Well, relative to you, the ball's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:55.860 --> 00:45:01.020
moving. But if you're watching the train go by,&nbsp;
the ball's moving at 60 miles an hour. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:06.000
the source of all of our problems is that we only&nbsp;
see that we're this person on the train holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:10.980
this ball, and we can't see that there's a person&nbsp;
outside who has a completely different view into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.980 --> 00:45:17.880
the exact same situation that we see. So we can&nbsp;
mentally use these tools to sort of accommodate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:25.140
how we can look into the world from a different&nbsp;
person's eyes or through a different lens, be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:34.080
that biology or through thinking or through — the&nbsp;
most useful ones tend to be like, "And then what?"&nbsp;

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:39.240
Just asking yourself that simple question, "I&nbsp;
accomplish this and then what happens?" Inversion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:44.760
"How do I avoid all these negative outcomes that I&nbsp;
don't want to get?" And the Stoics, you've talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:48.600
about this in the past, the Stoics have been doing&nbsp;
this for a long time. They call it a pre-mortem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:54.660
I think that those are just really helpful ways.&nbsp;
And what they're really doing is they're just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:54.660 --> 00:45:58.680
allowing us to put a different lens onto the&nbsp;
problem and see it through a different light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:03.167
And by seeing it through a different light, we&nbsp;
slightly reduce our blind spots into the problem.&nbsp;

00:46:03.167 --> 00:46:12.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally. I haven't read these&nbsp;
in decades, so caveat lector for anyone who picks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:17.400
these up. But some of Edward de Bono's&nbsp;
work, I think he's originally Maltese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:17.400 --> 00:46:25.380
so de Bono's two words, or actually he may&nbsp;
just be a Maltese citizen. I can't peg the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.380 --> 00:46:29.520
nationality. It doesn't really matter. He wrote a&nbsp;
book called Lateral Thinking. He has another one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.520 --> 00:46:32.700
I think it's called The Six Thinking&nbsp;
Hats or something along those lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:33.300 --> 00:46:40.740
which similarly, very helpful just for forcing&nbsp;
yourself as a portion of a practice to look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:44.520
same situation with a different lens, a different&nbsp;
set of questions, a different perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:45.240 --> 00:46:54.840
I'd love to talk to you about Farnam Street, but&nbsp;
more broadly building a business/life/businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.340 --> 00:47:05.460
on content, on advice, on writing, and&nbsp;
also certainly audio. But if you look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:13.740
back post-intelligence agency on what&nbsp;
you have built, what are some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:13.740 --> 00:47:20.460
more important decisions that you have made or&nbsp;
more important experiments that you have run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:20.460 --> 00:47:26.700
whether they turned out as you had hoped or not?
Shane Parrish: I think building a business on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:35.340
content is really hard. I think I never set out&nbsp;
to build a media company. What I set out to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:41.640
was learn timeless ideas from other people and&nbsp;
master the best of what other people have figured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:41.640 --> 00:47:47.100
out and share that with the world as a means of&nbsp;
giving back. And part of what I want to do in life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:53.400
is leave the world a better place than I found it.&nbsp;
And I believe in equal opportunity and not equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:53.400 --> 00:47:59.040
outcome. But how can we have equal opportunity if&nbsp;
we don't share information with people and make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:05.340
accessible? So that's how I stumbled into Farnam&nbsp;
Street. And there's been these pivotal moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:10.380
along the way, and some are intentional and some&nbsp;
are just completely random. An example of which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:15.660
I never had email. I never got people&nbsp;
to sign up for email until FeedBurner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:15.660 --> 00:48:18.660
Remember when FeedBurner went away?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I do, I do.&nbsp;

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:25.980
Shane Parrish: Right. So I lost three-quarters&nbsp;
of my audience overnight because Google decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:25.980 --> 00:48:30.780
to shut down this thing. And then at that&nbsp;
moment it dawned on me, "How can somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:37.440
else take away my audience from me? No, that's&nbsp;
not cool. How do I solve for this problem?" And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:43.380
so I started an email list and now we have&nbsp;
600,000 or 700,000 people on the email list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.380 --> 00:48:48.900
and I sent out an email every Sunday, basically&nbsp;
what I'm learning. But that was the moment. I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:53.820
never thought about, "How do I create an email&nbsp;
list?" Or, "I need this." It was like somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:59.100
took away my — Facebook has done the same thing.&nbsp;
Twitter to varying extents has done the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:04.680
thing. They basically take your audience away from&nbsp;
you and then you become platform-dependent. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:09.720
don't want to be platform-dependent. I don't&nbsp;
want anybody to be between me and the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:16.500
who've chosen to read me and get my ideas.
Tim Ferriss: So what are the components of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:21.540
your business as it exists today? And then I'm&nbsp;
happy to have a conversation about this. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:21.540 --> 00:49:25.500
just fascinated how different people approach&nbsp;
things, especially since we're talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:32.760
among other things, decision-making and so&nbsp;
on. So I'd love to know how it breaks down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:33.600 --> 00:49:37.260
currently because you've got online&nbsp;
courses, you have the podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:43.440
you have other things. How does the business,&nbsp;
so to speak, breakdown as it stands right now?&nbsp;

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:47.400
Shane Parrish: So we do a few things.&nbsp;
You remember that diagram from Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:51.780
in the '60s and they have theme parks and&nbsp;
merchandise and how it all interconnects?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:51.780 --> 00:49:53.880
You can look this up on the internet.
Tim Ferriss: I don't, but I would love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:54.467
to find it. All right, I'll find it.
Shane Parrish: So with Farnam Street —&nbsp;

00:49:54.467 --> 00:49:58.320
Tim Ferriss: And we'll put it in the show notes.
Shane Parrish: Yeah. So my idea with Farnam Street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.320 --> 00:50:05.040
was sort of, how do I create more surface area&nbsp;
for people to discover timeless content or that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:08.580
they master the best of what other people have&nbsp;
figured out? So it's literally not about me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:13.020
it's about other people's ideas. I don't&nbsp;
really have any original ideas. I sort of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:18.480
if anything, maybe compress other&nbsp;
people's ideas in an interesting way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:26.880
And so we started with a website and then we went&nbsp;
to a podcast and then the podcast, interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:32.520
side note here, Naval Ravikant saved the podcast&nbsp;
because I was doing this podcast and I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:36.600
"Nobody's listening to this podcast." And I had&nbsp;
just recorded with Naval. And I was like, "That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:42.723
the last episode I'm going to do." And then of&nbsp;
course that episode goes parabolic. And I'm like —&nbsp;

00:50:42.723 --> 00:50:45.840
Tim Ferriss: All roads lead to Naval.
Shane Parrish: And I was like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:51.240
my God, I can't give up on this anymore,"&nbsp;
because at the time, I wasn't having fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:51.240 --> 00:50:57.300
doing it. Now I'm having a lot of fun doing&nbsp;
it. Then we created these series of books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:57.300 --> 00:51:01.740
called The Great Mental Models. And the idea&nbsp;
behind The Great Mental Models was basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:08.100
what are the big ideas you would've learned in&nbsp;
university and can you apply those outside of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:08.100 --> 00:51:15.120
context of the domain that they were learned in?
And so I think that we've talked about one in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:15.120 --> 00:51:20.640
terms of the frame of reference that you're&nbsp;
using to look at a problem matters a lot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:26.640
terms of what you see. So we wanted to make that&nbsp;
accessible for people. And then we branched into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:31.860
course called Decision by Design. We used to have&nbsp;
an in-person workshop on decision making and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:37.680
learned a lot about how people make decisions&nbsp;
in the real world, but COVID shut down all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:45.180
these online events. And so we created Decision by&nbsp;
Design as a means to not take the same material we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:45.180 --> 00:51:49.560
were doing in person, because what we were doing&nbsp;
in person was very specific to being in person.&nbsp;

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:56.340
It was learning from the people at your table. It&nbsp;
was nontransferable in a way to online. So we took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:56.340 --> 00:52:00.900
a more prescriptive approach, which are like,&nbsp;
"What are the things, the practical tools that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:00.900 --> 00:52:05.760
people use in the real world to make decisions?"&nbsp;
And we share it with people. And an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:05.760 --> 00:52:12.780
that is separating problem definition from problem&nbsp;
solution, having two separate meetings for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:12.780 --> 00:52:16.920
And a lot of people go into these meetings&nbsp;
and they have one meeting and they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.920 --> 00:52:21.780
"What's the problem?" And then of course, we&nbsp;
work with incredibly intelligent people and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:21.780 --> 00:52:28.260
of them are type A. And so you come in and the&nbsp;
first problem statement that sounds reasonable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:33.360
everybody jumps into, "Okay, here's how we solve&nbsp;
for that." Nobody takes a step back and is like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:37.380
"Wait, is this problem worth solving?&nbsp;
Is this problem the real problem?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.460 --> 00:52:41.820
because we jump into these solutions.
Tim Ferriss: Could you give an example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:41.820 --> 00:52:48.480
could be hypothetical, could be&nbsp;
real-life, of scrutinizing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:49.740 --> 00:52:54.300
problem statement? I think this is really&nbsp;
important. I don't do it nearly as much as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:54.300 --> 00:53:00.420
I should. Could you maybe walk through what that&nbsp;
process looks like? If you have an example, great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:05.580
Otherwise, it could be in the more abstract.
Shane Parrish: So my belief is that the person who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:09.480
makes the decision has to come up with a problem&nbsp;
statement for the decision. And that does a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:14.220
things. That forces the person who's making the&nbsp;
decision to really understand the problem. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:14.220 --> 00:53:18.120
can take input from everybody else, but ultimately&nbsp;
they're accountable for taking the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:18.120 --> 00:53:25.620
statement and determining what that problem is&nbsp;
and communicating what that problem is, and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:25.620 --> 00:53:32.760
identifying which solutions work and fit for that.&nbsp;
When we don't do that and we just sort of throw a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:32.760 --> 00:53:45.780
problem on the table, it really backfires.
Tim Ferriss: I'll give you one on my own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:46.740 --> 00:53:54.600
So maybe we can workshop that. So for instance,&nbsp;
I have tons of legacy email addresses, inboxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:54.600 --> 00:54:02.400
and at least at face value, the story that I tell&nbsp;
is that it is a huge pain in the and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:09.420
different maybe security vulnerabilities and&nbsp;
issues with having different platforms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:09.420 --> 00:54:18.660
things scattered all over the place. The problem&nbsp;
in a sense has not been that well-defined. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:18.660 --> 00:54:27.300
just overall kind of angsty hand-wringing and&nbsp;
a couple of identified risks. Then we've jumped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:31.500
straight into solutions. And there are any host&nbsp;
of different solutions that I won't bore you with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:31.500 --> 00:54:38.100
but it's going to take some time. And I think&nbsp;
I have a pretty good beat on what the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.100 --> 00:54:47.580
direction is for this. But just for purposes of&nbsp;
using the exercise, how would you scrutinize that?&nbsp;

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:52.860
I come in, I say, "Hey, this is a problem. We&nbsp;
don't have things centralized in one modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:52.860 --> 00:55:00.000
inbox with all of the enterprise-level security&nbsp;
that that would include. Therefore, we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:01.020 --> 00:55:07.200
decommission these following inboxes and blah,&nbsp;
blah, blah." Right? But I'm taking for granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:11.880
that is the right definition of the problem.
Shane Parrish: Right, exactly. So it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:11.880 --> 00:55:16.080
who's the check and balance on, what are we&nbsp;
solving for? And it goes back to something we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:16.080 --> 00:55:20.940
talked about earlier. What direction? Am I getting&nbsp;
closer to the destination and is the destination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:20.940 --> 00:55:27.660
the one that I want to get to? So in this case, is&nbsp;
the problem that you're solving, "I want access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.660 --> 00:55:32.460
all of these materials through one inbox," or&nbsp;
is the problem something different, which is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:39.000
"I don't feel these inboxes are really that secure&nbsp;
and I don't know how to manage them." And the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:45.120
that you frame that problem will determine in a&nbsp;
large part what solutions you see as available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:49.067
And maybe it's something different. We're just&nbsp;
sort of spitballing this on the spot here.&nbsp;

00:55:49.067 --> 00:55:52.380
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.
Shane Parrish: But your surface area is large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.380 --> 00:55:58.500
right? So all these email addresses increase&nbsp;
your surface area of attack if that's the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:03.960
that you're thinking about it. And if your goal&nbsp;
is to reduce it, one way of reducing it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:09.300
be to consolidate it. One way of reducing it&nbsp;
might be to eliminate them. One way of reducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:13.440
it might be something completely different.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally. And this is timely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.440 --> 00:56:21.360
for me because as I was walking through recording&nbsp;
a screen flow for my team related to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.360 --> 00:56:25.740
I realized about halfway through, because I was&nbsp;
talking about next actions, and I said, "Actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:25.740 --> 00:56:31.500
wait a second, my ultimate objective here is&nbsp;
to spend substantially less time in email."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:32.700 --> 00:56:39.960
And I think maybe compared to a reference point&nbsp;
that would be, say, an average of my friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:45.120
I do spend a lot less time in email, but I would&nbsp;
like to spend more time on certain creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:45.120 --> 00:56:53.520
projects and larger needle-moving dedicated blocks&nbsp;
of time, three to four hours for fill-in-the-blank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.520 --> 00:57:01.740
project or experiment. I realized pretty quickly&nbsp;
that if my advice were implemented directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:07.080
it would just be taking this burden. It's not just&nbsp;
attack vectors, it's also just lots of unsolicited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:07.080 --> 00:57:13.020
inbound. It would be taking all of that and&nbsp;
just moving it to a different mailing address.&nbsp;

00:57:13.020 --> 00:57:23.760
And I was like, "Okay, that partially solves&nbsp;
one issue, but it does not at all address this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.760 --> 00:57:27.840
separate issue, which is maybe on some level the&nbsp;
bigger issue, even though I tend to run a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:33.720
paranoid, the unsolicited inbound is probably a&nbsp;
bigger problem currently than the attack factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:39.240
stuff." I have pretty good security as it stands,&nbsp;
probably excessive security in a lot of respects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:40.560 --> 00:57:45.660
But I realized halfway through trying&nbsp;
to prescribe the solution, I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.660 --> 00:57:51.585
"Wait a fucking second. I might be giving the&nbsp;
best advice possible for solving the wrong thing."&nbsp;

00:57:51.585 --> 00:57:56.340
Shane Parrish: Yeah, totally. Right. And&nbsp;
that is in and of itself a good reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.340 --> 00:58:00.900
separate these things. But also when you're&nbsp;
the boss, your suggestions become direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.740 --> 00:58:07.380
So you might be just thinking out loud, but the&nbsp;
people hearing that are like, "Oh, Tim wants this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.380 --> 00:58:11.460
Here's how we solve for this." And then in your&nbsp;
head you're like, "Oh, that sounds like a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:11.460 --> 00:58:17.040
solution," but you've solved the wrong problem.&nbsp;
And so this is why separating these meetings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:21.900
between what is the problem, can we get clear on&nbsp;
what the problem is? And here's what — you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:21.900 --> 00:58:26.520
have to go first, but whatever your process&nbsp;
is to get that information out to people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:30.840
terms of what they see as the problem, you can&nbsp;
take that, mull it over and be like, "We'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.840 --> 00:58:36.660
have another meeting tomorrow and we'll determine&nbsp;
solutions. Or maybe next week if it's not timely."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.660 --> 00:58:41.040
And people think this slows you down, this is the&nbsp;
biggest thing that I hear when I talk to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:45.000
about this is they're like, "Well, that'll just&nbsp;
slow us down and prevent us from taking action."&nbsp;

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:50.700
Actually, the exact opposite is true because it&nbsp;
increases — it's sort of like you're thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:50.700 --> 00:58:54.960
in terms of speed, but speed doesn't have&nbsp;
a direction. It doesn't matter how fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:58.440
you're going. If you're running in circles,&nbsp;
you really want to get to the best outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:58.440 --> 00:59:02.940
possible in the shortest amount of time with&nbsp;
the least amount of effort. And that means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:02.940 --> 00:59:07.260
you're allowing your team to maximize their&nbsp;
thinking. A lot of people don't think well on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:07.260 --> 00:59:11.760
the spot. So having these group meetings where&nbsp;
everybody's required to contribute — a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:15.600
people mull it over. They go for a walk after,&nbsp;
or they're walking home after work that night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:15.600 --> 00:59:18.960
and then they get this idea, this spark and&nbsp;
they're like, "No, I don't see it that way."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:23.880
And then they can't contribute at that point. So&nbsp;
you've tied one hand behind your back in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:29.400
of maximizing the talent on your team to figure&nbsp;
out what the problem is and then to solve it.&nbsp;

00:59:29.400 --> 00:59:34.440
And so it makes a lot more sense to be like, "Hey,&nbsp;
we're going to have a 30-minute meeting here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:34.440 --> 00:59:38.940
a 30-minute meeting here instead of one 60-minute&nbsp;
meeting, and these meetings are going to be highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:43.020
structured and we're just going to talk about&nbsp;
what the problem is. Can we agree on what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:43.020 --> 00:59:49.620
problem is? What do you see as the problem? What&nbsp;
do you see into this lens that I don't see? I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:49.620 --> 00:59:55.260
not you. I can't see it." And that's a way also&nbsp;
to get out of this — I don't know. People like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:55.260 --> 01:00:00.120
to posture in meetings. It drives me crazy. They&nbsp;
basically repeat the executive summary in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:05.100
own words and meetings go nowhere. And so a good&nbsp;
way to avoid that is just to assume that everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:05.100 --> 01:00:11.760
has read the required material and ask them what&nbsp;
their unique contribution to this problem is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:17.400
they don't think anybody else sees. And so now&nbsp;
instead of rewarding just airtime, now you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:17.400 --> 01:00:23.400
rewarding unique contribution to the problem and&nbsp;
you get social signal out of having a unique lens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:23.400 --> 01:00:28.320
into the problem, which is much more effective.
Tim Ferriss: That makes perfect sense. And are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:28.320 --> 01:00:34.080
there other questions that you find useful in&nbsp;
the problem-defining sessions? For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:34.860 --> 01:00:41.820
I can imagine one of the questions that&nbsp;
I would want to ask my team in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:41.820 --> 01:00:47.280
to emphasizing what you just emphasized,&nbsp;
individual perspective and contribution over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:48.240 --> 01:00:55.020
restating the executive summary that was sent&nbsp;
as reading for the meeting to begin with. Is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:55.020 --> 01:01:00.480
this really a problem? We're assuming this is&nbsp;
a problem that needs to be solved, but is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:01.920 --> 01:01:07.920
significant enough for us to allocate attention&nbsp;
and resources to it compared to other things?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:09.540 --> 01:01:14.340
I'm probably using very clumsy wording,&nbsp;
but what other types of — are there any&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:14.340 --> 01:01:17.940
other questions that you've&nbsp;
seen be very helpful in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:18.960 --> 01:01:22.380
problem-defining meetings?
Shane Parrish: "And then what?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:22.380 --> 01:01:24.045
is a great question, right?
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.&nbsp;

01:01:24.045 --> 01:01:28.680
Shane Parrish: "If we switch to a new email&nbsp;
platform, and then what?" You can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:28.680 --> 01:01:33.540
you can do a thought experiment on what your&nbsp;
day would look like, but we often neglect all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.540 --> 01:01:38.940
these other things, which is like, "This software&nbsp;
doesn't do this thing that I actually do a lot. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:43.620
therefore now we have to develop a workaround or&nbsp;
the software's completely different and everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.620 --> 01:01:48.180
has to be trained on it or the software's&nbsp;
not as supported and doesn't update as much."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:48.720 --> 01:01:52.980
There's all these lenses. But I love the, "And&nbsp;
then what" question because it's like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:59.880
if we solve this problem, what state are we in&nbsp;
and what is the cost in terms of time, money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.880 --> 01:02:04.320
effort, brain drain?" We don't think about&nbsp;
brain drain very often to solve this problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:04.320 --> 01:02:09.960
And the bigger your team is, the more you tend to&nbsp;
solve these problems that aren't really problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:16.020
because you're like creating busy work for you.
And I think we both run very small teams. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:16.020 --> 01:02:20.580
so we think through, "Okay, if we solve this&nbsp;
problem, then what happens? How do we develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:25.680
the most leverage possible?" But I think a lot&nbsp;
of people don't tend to think through that. I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:30.900
seen this all the time where organizations I've&nbsp;
worked with have switched out software and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:30.900 --> 01:02:38.100
switch out the software to solve one problem and&nbsp;
they're ignorant to the 67 other problems they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:38.100 --> 01:02:44.040
just created by solving that one problem. And&nbsp;
it feels good. It feels like you're doing work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.040 --> 01:02:48.660
but at the end of the day, you're in a&nbsp;
worse position than you were in because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:54.660
you didn't ask yourself, "And then what?"
Tim Ferriss: All right. I'd love to segue&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:54.660 --> 01:03:00.540
for a minute to automatic rules because for myself&nbsp;
at least, especially running a small team where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:09.180
the default, I suppose, flow of things is&nbsp;
that a lot of decisions flow to me and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:12.780
try not to be an unnecessary bottleneck, but&nbsp;
I have a very small team, a few employees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:19.980
and automatic rules and policies are very helpful&nbsp;
here. For instance, I have a blog post I put up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:26.280
it's like I'm not reading any new books this year,&nbsp;
which solves a lot of issues as you might imagine.&nbsp;

01:03:26.280 --> 01:03:35.520
And I have these blanket policies for at least&nbsp;
five to eight years. I did no speaking engagements&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.520 --> 01:03:40.200
effectively. I've made a few exceptions recently,&nbsp;
but for a long period of time I did none of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:40.200 --> 01:03:45.540
For another, I think I want to say probably&nbsp;
three to four years, no angel investing. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:45.540 --> 01:03:53.940
these were rules that prevented decision fatigue&nbsp;
and just allowed me a lot more cognitive bandwidth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:53.940 --> 01:03:59.640
and emotional and physical bandwidth. So what are&nbsp;
some of your automatic rules? I've got a few here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:59.640 --> 01:04:05.700
work out every day, no days off, invest in an&nbsp;
index fund every month, no meetings before 12.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:05.700 --> 01:04:10.860
Do you want to spend maybe a few minutes on those&nbsp;
and then any other examples that come to mind?&nbsp;

01:04:10.860 --> 01:04:15.540
Shane Parrish: Before I get into a few&nbsp;
more examples, let's back up for a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:16.500 --> 01:04:22.560
Automatic rules turn your desired behavior into&nbsp;
your default behavior, and they do it when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:22.560 --> 01:04:29.580
at your best. And so you can pre-decide a lot of&nbsp;
things. And why are rules effective instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:29.580 --> 01:04:36.360
just a pre-decision? You need it to be a rule. And&nbsp;
the word rule is very specific and very useful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.020 --> 01:04:44.280
And I got this idea from Daniel Kahneman and we&nbsp;
were talking and he said, "I have a rule. I never&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:52.500
say 'Yes' on the phone." And he was explaining why&nbsp;
he had just told somebody that he'd talk to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:56.760
again tomorrow and follow up with them and they&nbsp;
had asked him to do something. And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:56.760 --> 01:05:01.740
"That's really interesting. Why do you have&nbsp;
that rule?" And he said, "That rule is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:05.580
that I don't end up saying 'Yes,' because I&nbsp;
feel social pressure to say 'Yes' to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:10.020
things and I want to please other people."
All of us want to please other people. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:16.980
he's like, "I learned over years..." and this is&nbsp;
fascinating, will blow your mind, will change your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:24.000
life. He's like, "I learned over years, people&nbsp;
don't argue with rules and I follow them." So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:28.680
we're taught our whole life to follow rules,&nbsp;
but we've never thought of how can we create&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:28.680 --> 01:05:34.860
rules for ourself that we just follow when we're&nbsp;
at our worst when we're in the worst situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.860 --> 01:05:38.940
possible? We all know no matter how bad our day&nbsp;
is, we shouldn't speed on the highway because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:38.940 --> 01:05:45.600
it's against the rules. What we don't think&nbsp;
about is how do we use rules to our advantage?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:53.100
A great example, I had a friend who basically&nbsp;
lost a whole bunch of weight using this rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.100 --> 01:05:59.460
and he basically said, "Every time I go out for&nbsp;
dinner..." and he ate out for dinner a lot in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:59.460 --> 01:06:05.880
life for work. He's like, "My rule is I order the&nbsp;
healthiest thing on the menu and I don't drink."&nbsp;

01:06:05.880 --> 01:06:09.330
Tim Ferriss: Meaning no alcohol.
Shane Parrish: Meaning no alcohol, right?&nbsp;

01:06:09.330 --> 01:06:12.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.
Shane Parrish: And he didn't order dessert.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:16.320
That was his third rule. And if you think about&nbsp;
dessert, dessert's an interesting one because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:16.320 --> 01:06:20.100
think we can all relate to it. You go out, you&nbsp;
celebrate with your friends, the wine comes out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:20.100 --> 01:06:23.520
the champagne comes out, the dessert comes&nbsp;
out, and all of a sudden you're doing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:27.960
that maybe the best version of you doesn't&nbsp;
want to do, but you're in a social situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:27.960 --> 01:06:33.840
you feel pressure. You can tell people, I'm not&nbsp;
eating dessert, I don't want dessert tonight. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:33.840 --> 01:06:38.940
that relies on willpower. So every time you&nbsp;
say, "I don't want dessert tonight," you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:38.940 --> 01:06:44.220
using willpower to get out of that situation.&nbsp;
And eventually, everybody loses the battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:44.220 --> 01:06:48.960
with willpower. So how can you create a system to&nbsp;
get out of this willpower thing where it's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:54.000
"I'm not using willpower, I create an automatic&nbsp;
rule. I don't eat dessert. That's my rule."&nbsp;

01:06:54.000 --> 01:06:58.560
And when you say that to your friends, you might&nbsp;
have to say it twice, but they definitely push&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.560 --> 01:07:03.840
back a lot less as long as you're consistent&nbsp;
with that rule. But that rule puts you in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.840 --> 01:07:10.440
best position possible for success. And I&nbsp;
have a couple of these rules. When I am out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:10.440 --> 01:07:15.060
after a talk with the people who organize the&nbsp;
talk, my rule is I stopped drinking at 9:00,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:22.200
nothing could happen after 9:00. And so you just&nbsp;
choose in advance. And a great way to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:22.200 --> 01:07:27.540
this again is going back to what we talked about&nbsp;
earlier, direction, destination. What does a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:27.540 --> 01:07:33.000
person who achieves this destination, what do they&nbsp;
behave like? Can I create automatic rules in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:33.000 --> 01:07:36.600
life? And they're so context-specific,&nbsp;
they're so specific to you because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.600 --> 01:07:43.680
they're also things you don't do naturally.
I hate working out, but I want to be healthy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.680 --> 01:07:48.270
It's really important to me to be healthy. And&nbsp;
I was struggling, and this is where the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:48.270 --> 01:07:52.860
out every day rule came in. I was struggling to&nbsp;
go three times a week because I would get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:52.860 --> 01:08:00.720
this negotiation with myself. And that negotiation&nbsp;
was like, "I normally work out on Wednesdays," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.720 --> 01:08:04.920
that little voice in my head is like, "You didn't&nbsp;
sleep well last night. You have a lot of work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:04.920 --> 01:08:10.320
your plate." And then all of a sudden I'm like,&nbsp;
"I'll do extra tomorrow." I'll catch myself doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:11.520 --> 01:08:17.160
this sort of talk and I'm, "Why am I negotiating&nbsp;
with myself? Who's in charge here? I'm in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:17.160 --> 01:08:22.560
of this situation." And so I changed it and&nbsp;
I'm like, "I am going to work out every day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:22.560 --> 01:08:27.300
And it doesn't mean I go to the gym every day&nbsp;
and it doesn't mean I go for 60 minutes. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:27.300 --> 01:08:34.020
reduce the scope or duration of my workout,&nbsp;
but I'm going to work out every single day."&nbsp;

01:08:34.020 --> 01:08:37.920
And that workout might be a five-mile walk,&nbsp;
it might be something else, it doesn't matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.920 --> 01:08:44.820
But there's going to be some form of exercise&nbsp;
involved. And that single rule has changed so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:44.820 --> 01:08:51.120
many people's lives that I've shared it with you&nbsp;
because you start exercising every day because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:57.780
negotiation in your head now is like, "What do I&nbsp;
fit in today? How can I fit this in?" It's not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:57.780 --> 01:09:02.040
"Should I work out?" It's like, "Oh, I don't have&nbsp;
a lot of time. Maybe I'll just do 20 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:02.040 --> 01:09:07.800
Maybe I'll just go to the gym and do squats."&nbsp;
And all of a sudden you get in this habit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:07.800 --> 01:09:13.680
you're consistently working out, so your health&nbsp;
starts to get a lot better. It's mind-blowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.680 --> 01:09:18.636
how this simple principle can have profound&nbsp;
impact. Do you have any rules for yourself?&nbsp;

01:09:18.636 --> 01:09:22.067
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I have so many.
Shane Parrish: I want to hear some of yours.&nbsp;

01:09:22.067 --> 01:09:28.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Well, I will say that as&nbsp;
you're talking, my father for instance has in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:28.200 --> 01:09:33.660
the last — it's been very fast actually, maybe in&nbsp;
the last two to three months has lost 50 pounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.200 --> 01:09:41.460
And he's done that by following the slow-carb&nbsp;
diet in The 4-Hour Body as described by The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:47.040
4-Hour Body. People can find it online, certainly&nbsp;
without having to buy any book. But the key there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:54.420
one of the keys, is having a very straightforward&nbsp;
rule. And the rule is you don't have to give up&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.420 --> 01:09:57.720
your favorite foods for the rest of your life.&nbsp;
You just have to give up your favorite foods&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:57.720 --> 01:10:03.240
for six days at a time and you have a set cheat&nbsp;
day every week. And that also allows you to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.240 --> 01:10:08.760
off the hook or to successfully negotiate with&nbsp;
yourself or with your friends because you say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:12.720
"It's my policy that I only have&nbsp;
cheat day once a week on Saturday."&nbsp;

01:10:12.720 --> 01:10:17.520
So if you want to have dessert, booze, whatever&nbsp;
it might be — not in my dad's case with those two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:17.520 --> 01:10:22.560
but I'm happy to do it, but it's got to be on a&nbsp;
Saturday. And then all of a sudden the negotiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:22.560 --> 01:10:29.320
is over. There is no negotiation. I would say&nbsp;
when I am my best self, which I am right now —&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.060 --> 01:10:34.620
let's see. Another rule would be if I want&nbsp;
to follow the slow-carb diet, have pre-made&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:34.620 --> 01:10:39.000
meals ready to go in the refrigerator for at&nbsp;
least three to four days at a time. So I did&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:44.520
that today for instance, because if I start to&nbsp;
run out of food and I'm picking at the dregs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:44.520 --> 01:10:48.780
whatever I have sitting around and I'm starting to&nbsp;
starve, but lo and behold I have a podcast to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:48.780 --> 01:10:52.800
I'm going to go buy something fast that&nbsp;
is not going to conform to the diet.&nbsp;

01:10:54.060 --> 01:11:02.100
Batch-processing buying or having bought food&nbsp;
so that it's ready to go. I mean I have a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:02.100 --> 01:11:05.940
of policies. So as it relates to inbound,&nbsp;
and I have an autoresponder that kicks back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:05.940 --> 01:11:09.600
a lot of this stuff, I'll tell you some of&nbsp;
the rules that have existed for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:09.600 --> 01:11:14.520
generally speaking, no speaking engagements,&nbsp;
and I could explain why that is. That was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:14.520 --> 01:11:20.820
particularly hard one when I made the decision&nbsp;
because speaking engagements for authors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:20.820 --> 01:11:27.240
have bestselling books is very lucrative and it&nbsp;
is, in fact, the main source of income for a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:27.240 --> 01:11:36.780
of authors. And I just realized categorically&nbsp;
I had to say "No" because it was very hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:36.780 --> 01:11:41.700
moderate my commitments with speaking engagements.
Shane Parrish: Yeah. And it's addictive, right?&nbsp;

01:11:41.700 --> 01:11:43.200
Tim Ferriss: It is addictive.
Shane Parrish: You hop on a plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:43.200 --> 01:11:45.900
you get this big check, and then&nbsp;
you could do it all over again.&nbsp;

01:11:45.900 --> 01:11:51.660
Tim Ferriss: And then you end up booking your&nbsp;
life out nine months in advance and it crowds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:51.660 --> 01:11:57.600
out a lot of other things. Like the metaphor of&nbsp;
this apocryphal story of — the professor asks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:57.600 --> 01:12:03.360
the students to fit the two rocks and a handful&nbsp;
of pebbles and then the sand into the jar. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:07.980
the lesson is you've got to put in the big rocks&nbsp;
first and then the pebbles and then the sand. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:07.980 --> 01:12:11.640
you can't do it the other way around. You can't&nbsp;
do it in any other order or the big rocks won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:11.640 --> 01:12:16.080
fit. And I realized for myself that was true with&nbsp;
speaking engagements. What I did do as an interim&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.080 --> 01:12:22.320
for that is when I would say yes to speaking&nbsp;
engagements, I had a complete barbell approach.&nbsp;

01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:27.180
So I would either do free, so it had to be so&nbsp;
incredibly compelling. Maybe that's because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:33.120
it's cause-based and really aligned with a very&nbsp;
narrow band of things that I commit myself to on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:33.120 --> 01:12:42.420
that side, or it had to be a paid gig matching or&nbsp;
above my previous high watermark. So whatever the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:42.420 --> 01:12:49.560
highest amount that I had been paid was, it had&nbsp;
to match or exceed that never below. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:49.560 --> 01:12:54.180
automatically constrained the volume of things&nbsp;
that I could commit to and made it manageable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:54.720 --> 01:12:59.820
And by the way, the people who are willing to do&nbsp;
that were always the easiest to deal with. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:04.560
would think maybe intuitively that the people who&nbsp;
pay the most would be the most demanding and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:04.560 --> 01:13:08.400
most difficult. That has not been my experience&nbsp;
at all. It's actually completely the opposite.&nbsp;

01:13:10.620 --> 01:13:14.280
I have many other rules. So the "No&nbsp;
new books" would be another one. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:14.280 --> 01:13:19.560
anyone tries to rush me into a decision via&nbsp;
email or on the phone, the answer is "No."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.220 --> 01:13:24.600
It's not just that I postpone giving an answer,&nbsp;
the answer's "No." If I feel like someone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.600 --> 01:13:28.860
rushing me into a decision, the answer is "No."&nbsp;
If someone's rushing me because reading between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:28.860 --> 01:13:32.880
the lines, they don't want me to have enough time&nbsp;
for a proper legal review — a lot of startups do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:32.880 --> 01:13:38.160
this. It's very annoying where they'll send&nbsp;
you docs on Friday afternoon and say, "Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:38.160 --> 01:13:45.240
we need to close this round and we need to get&nbsp;
signatures back by 10:00 a.m. on Monday." And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:45.240 --> 01:13:49.020
like, "Then it's a fucking 'No,' I'm sorry.&nbsp;
I'm an adult and I don't play these games."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.400 --> 01:13:55.740
So there are a handful of things like that.
I'll give you another one for the podcast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:55.740 --> 01:14:00.480
I'm going to ask you a similar question for&nbsp;
the business side of things for you and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:00.480 --> 01:14:05.160
creative side, but I'd love to hear any. So for&nbsp;
instance, for the podcast, everything is prepaid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:05.160 --> 01:14:12.000
all invoices are prepaid, and that is something&nbsp;
that very few podcasts can do. It's something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:12.000 --> 01:14:19.260
very few podcasts can manage given the business&nbsp;
norms. Generally, it's net 30 or net 60 or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.260 --> 01:14:25.620
type of net payment terms. But to manage that,&nbsp;
you either need to have a reasonably large team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:25.620 --> 01:14:30.540
you need to have someone fully dedicated to&nbsp;
chasing down payment or you need to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:30.540 --> 01:14:35.400
outside agencies to pick up that burden. I didn't&nbsp;
want to deal with any of that. And so I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:35.400 --> 01:14:40.200
"Look, this is..." I recognize a tradeoff. And&nbsp;
with these rules, I think you would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:40.200 --> 01:14:43.560
agree there are tradeoffs. There&nbsp;
always are going to be trade-offs.&nbsp;

01:14:43.560 --> 01:14:49.020
But if decision is like de-cision, kind of like&nbsp;
incision, like the cutting away, that's fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.020 --> 01:14:54.120
So you're cutting away certain possible outcomes.&nbsp;
But if I make a rule that everyone has to prepay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:55.020 --> 01:14:59.760
a few things happen. Number one, the universe&nbsp;
of possible sponsors we need to consider goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:59.760 --> 01:15:04.860
down dramatically. Secondly, the people who are&nbsp;
willing to do that, again, counterintuitively, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:04.860 --> 01:15:09.060
generally people who are measuring twice, cutting&nbsp;
once, and are going to be the easiest to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:09.060 --> 01:15:15.660
later. And we don't always bat a thousand, but&nbsp;
we do pretty well. So those would be some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:18.840
rules. I've got all sorts of other ones.
Shane Parrish: As you were saying that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:24.480
there's a couple of things that came to mind.&nbsp;
One is, I can often determine from an initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:24.480 --> 01:15:30.540
outreach of an investment whether I want to&nbsp;
do the deal before I even know the specifics&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:30.540 --> 01:15:35.700
because it's like, "How fair is this to me?"&nbsp;
And if the deal is one-sided towards you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:37.020 --> 01:15:41.460
an example of which is like, "Hey, I need&nbsp;
this by…" you can't get it legally reviewed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:41.460 --> 01:15:45.360
or you have to get your lawyers working overtime,&nbsp;
"I need it by Monday." Well, I don't want to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:45.360 --> 01:15:47.520
partner with that person. And so that's —
Tim Ferriss: That's the honeymoon. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:47.520 --> 01:15:48.347
the honeymoon phase.
Shane Parrish: I know.&nbsp;

01:15:48.347 --> 01:15:51.060
Tim Ferriss: Should be easier.
Shane Parrish: So that's an easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:51.060 --> 01:15:55.680
"No." But often when we're investing in a lot&nbsp;
of private businesses that aren't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:55.680 --> 01:15:59.880
startups that are profitable businesses,&nbsp;
I can tell by the valuation, the pitch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:59.880 --> 01:16:04.080
what people are looking for, what their optimal&nbsp;
scenario is, if I want to be a partner with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:04.080 --> 01:16:09.360
because if you're presenting a deal that isn't&nbsp;
fair to me right now, you're never going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:09.360 --> 01:16:14.340
fair to me in the future. It has to be win-win.&nbsp;
And people don't understand. There's literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.340 --> 01:16:20.520
four permutations of a relationship. There's&nbsp;
win-win, win-lose, lose-win, and lose-lose. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:20.520 --> 01:16:26.400
only one of those permutations can survive time.&nbsp;
And what do we want with time? We want to compound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:26.400 --> 01:16:30.720
relationships. You want to invest in companies&nbsp;
where you don't have to review the legal documents&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:30.720 --> 01:16:35.340
because you trust the people involved, you've&nbsp;
worked with them before, they're consistent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:35.340 --> 01:16:40.200
they show up, they do what they say they're going&nbsp;
to do, and maybe the investment doesn't work out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:40.200 --> 01:16:43.740
but that's not what you're thinking, but you're&nbsp;
not worried about them stabbing you in the back.&nbsp;

01:16:43.740 --> 01:16:49.980
And so often we do things that just take us off&nbsp;
this compounding train, and I think it was Naval&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:49.980 --> 01:16:55.440
who said, "One of the big lessons from compounding&nbsp;
is that all the gains come at the end. They don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:55.440 --> 01:17:01.620
come at the start." And so you have to stay on&nbsp;
this timeline. So every relationship has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:01.620 --> 01:17:08.820
win-win. And for the podcast we do like no paid&nbsp;
speaking, which is — it's so interesting. I'm sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:08.820 --> 01:17:12.000
you get these pitches too. It's like, "Here,&nbsp;
I'll give you 100 grand to come on your show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:12.000 --> 01:17:16.800
I'll pay in advance, yada yada." And then I&nbsp;
always look up these people and I'm like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:16.800 --> 01:17:22.260
now I know the podcasts that accept money for&nbsp;
guest speakers." But we do the same thing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:22.260 --> 01:17:26.820
this prepaid stuff, and this came back to bug&nbsp;
us because I'm a high-trust person. I operate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:26.820 --> 01:17:31.500
on trust. I don't want to be in business with&nbsp;
you if we need a contract. That's generally my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:35.820
motto. It doesn't mean we don't have contracts,&nbsp;
but it means if I feel the need of a contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:35.820 --> 01:17:39.000
it's an easy "No" for me. And so —
Tim Ferriss: If you need a contract to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:43.785
protect you from someone you don't really&nbsp;
trust, you're playing a gambler's game.&nbsp;

01:17:43.785 --> 01:17:46.920
Shane Parrish: But often you don't&nbsp;
even think that you don't trust them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:46.920 --> 01:17:50.760
but you'll think of the contract and then you'll&nbsp;
need a contract. And that's the first indication&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.760 --> 01:17:56.100
that something's amiss in this relationship.&nbsp;
And so, we've never used advertising contracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:57.060 --> 01:18:02.520
but during COVID, this came back to bite us&nbsp;
because we would invoice a lot of our advertisers&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:02.520 --> 01:18:10.380
once a year and we invoiced them on March 31st —&nbsp;
after we have delivered all of their advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.380 --> 01:18:14.280
for the past year. And we've worked with&nbsp;
people for years and then all of a sudden we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.280 --> 01:18:19.620
delivering these invoices March 31st, 2020. And&nbsp;
people are like, "We're not paying that." And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.620 --> 01:18:23.520
like, "Whoa, what do you mean we're not paying&nbsp;
this? That doesn't make any sense. We've worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:23.520 --> 01:18:28.800
together for four years. How is this even&nbsp;
possible? Am I missing something?" And they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:28.800 --> 01:18:33.000
like, "Basically, sue me." And I'm like, "Ooh."
Tim Ferriss: Fun.&nbsp;

01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:37.740
Shane Parrish: "What is going on here? This&nbsp;
is crazy." Interestingly enough, a few of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:41.220
have come back and tried to advertise with us&nbsp;
and they're like, "We'll pay our old invoice."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:41.220 --> 01:18:45.180
And I'm like, "You should pay that and then&nbsp;
we'll talk, but there's no way I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:45.180 --> 01:18:48.060
sign up to work with you again."
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. No, thanks.&nbsp;

01:18:48.060 --> 01:18:52.080
Shane Parrish: The speaking thing — again, that&nbsp;
comes back to, "Where do I want to get to in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:52.080 --> 01:18:56.820
life?" I do the exact same thing except I sort of&nbsp;
limit it. I'm like, "Here's the number of speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:56.820 --> 01:19:02.160
engagements I can do this year. It's like six&nbsp;
or 10 depending on the year, depending on what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:02.160 --> 01:19:08.880
I want to do that year." And then we use price as&nbsp;
a function to modulate that in the sense of — I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:08.880 --> 01:19:12.780
love your high watermark idea. I think that's&nbsp;
really important, and that's a great way that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:12.780 --> 01:19:17.880
had never thought about doing it, and I just use,&nbsp;
"Hey, I only do six a year. Here's the cost." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:17.880 --> 01:19:23.340
it just eliminates most people, and to your point,&nbsp;
the people that say "Yes" are the easiest freaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:23.340 --> 01:19:31.080
people to deal with in the world, and they want&nbsp;
you there and there's no 16 hours of pre-calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:31.080 --> 01:19:33.443
and all of this other stuff.
Tim Ferriss: No.&nbsp;

01:19:33.443 --> 01:19:34.140
Shane Parrish: Yeah.
Tim Ferriss: No. They've decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:34.140 --> 01:19:39.240
they want you as a speaker. You're not one of 10.&nbsp;
They will gladly accept. That's when you get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:39.240 --> 01:19:48.600
all sorts of comms overload and brain damage. If&nbsp;
you had to double down on one or two things you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:48.600 --> 01:19:53.340
are already doing, what would you double down on?
Shane Parrish: Oh, definitely the email list. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:53.340 --> 01:19:59.640
think that that is the key, right? If you can&nbsp;
pick one primary focus for this, you need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:59.640 --> 01:20:03.600
able to reach your audience, you need to be able&nbsp;
to communicate with them. And however you do that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:03.600 --> 01:20:09.600
whether you do it building it through Twitter&nbsp;
or Instagram, you want all past to lead back&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:09.600 --> 01:20:14.398
to your email list. And the person who does&nbsp;
this the best is James Clear. He constantly —&nbsp;

01:20:14.398 --> 01:20:17.220
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he is a machine.
Shane Parrish: He's a machine, right? Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.220 --> 01:20:22.380
points back to his website, Facebook points&nbsp;
back to his website, Twitter — he's just really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:22.380 --> 01:20:27.180
really smart at this. So if you're going to model&nbsp;
yourself off somebody and you want to build an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:27.180 --> 01:20:32.880
email list, he's the person to sort of think&nbsp;
about. You also have a huge audience for email.&nbsp;

01:20:32.880 --> 01:20:37.920
Tim Ferriss: I do, yes. I'm very, very grateful.&nbsp;
Very methodical too. I've thought about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:42.840
Although, like you, didn't think about it at&nbsp;
all until a few things happened, including feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:42.840 --> 01:20:49.980
burner, sending a notification, "We were about&nbsp;
to turn off everything," and then began emailing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:49.980 --> 01:20:55.860
however long ago it was, which has been a great,&nbsp;
actually a really fun process for me because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:55.860 --> 01:20:59.567
5-Bullet Friday serves as my diary effectively.
Shane Parrish: Yes, totally.&nbsp;

01:20:59.567 --> 01:21:03.300
Tim Ferriss: I don't have a separate diary, so&nbsp;
it's fun to look back two years ago. It's like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.300 --> 01:21:07.140
there's one that comes out every week and I&nbsp;
can look back two years and go, "Oh, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:07.140 --> 01:21:12.840
I remember that." That exact week's locked&nbsp;
in the amber. It's like some prehistoric&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.840 --> 01:21:18.240
mosquito or something, and I can revisit.
Shane Parrish: And you also realize how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:18.240 --> 01:21:22.200
stupid you were, or at least I do when I look&nbsp;
back at some of my old newsletters, I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:22.200 --> 01:21:26.760
"Oh, what was that?" But that's also a really&nbsp;
positive sign because you're growing. But going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.760 --> 01:21:33.000
back to the question, I would focus on email&nbsp;
and I would ditch podcasts at this point and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:33.000 --> 01:21:37.140
just double down on email and long-form&nbsp;
content counterintuitively on the web.&nbsp;

01:21:37.140 --> 01:21:41.640
Tim Ferriss: How would you do — can you give&nbsp;
— let's just say, I was like, wish granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:41.640 --> 01:21:48.000
boom, poof podcast just dissolves into the&nbsp;
ether with a rainbow and some whinnying of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:48.000 --> 01:21:53.100
unicorns and it's all wonderful, but that has&nbsp;
come to a close and then you're doubling down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.100 --> 01:21:59.294
on email and — what was the phrasing that&nbsp;
you just used? But it was sort of online —&nbsp;

01:21:59.294 --> 01:22:02.160
Shane Parrish: Long-form content.
Tim Ferriss: Long-form content. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:02.160 --> 01:22:06.540
would you do that? What might things&nbsp;
look like in the next three months if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.540 --> 01:22:10.500
you were doubling down on those things?
Shane Parrish: Yeah, so just for context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:10.500 --> 01:22:14.220
I was operating under the&nbsp;
guise of starting from scratch.&nbsp;

01:22:14.220 --> 01:22:17.040
Tim Ferriss: Okay. Got it.
Shane Parrish: I would pick a vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:17.040 --> 01:22:23.220
and that vector would be sort of creating&nbsp;
content that people find useful and timeless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:23.220 --> 01:22:28.200
and it's super important. The word timeless&nbsp;
is really important there because you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:28.200 --> 01:22:33.180
the compounding effect. You want the cumulative&nbsp;
effect to work to your advantage. So if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:33.180 --> 01:22:39.900
creating content that is easily consumed but no&nbsp;
longer relevant in a week, a month, even a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:39.900 --> 01:22:45.360
then that content can't work for you when you're&nbsp;
not working. But a lot of the stuff that you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:45.360 --> 01:22:51.900
on your blog, and definitely the stuff that we&nbsp;
do on FS Blog is — it's always relevant. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:51.900 --> 01:22:58.320
evergreen. We just re-aired that interview with&nbsp;
Naval Ravikant. We recorded it six years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:58.320 --> 01:23:04.140
People thought it was recorded yesterday. We don't&nbsp;
talk politics. We avoid these certain subjects.&nbsp;

01:23:04.140 --> 01:23:08.580
I think what I would do is I would focus on&nbsp;
creating the best content I could. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:08.580 --> 01:23:12.360
get people who come to the website to give me&nbsp;
their email address, and I would think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:12.360 --> 01:23:18.480
not expanding that surface area, just creating&nbsp;
better, "How do I write the next article? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:18.480 --> 01:23:21.960
do I make it useful?" And then spending a lot of&nbsp;
time thinking about, "How do I share that? Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:21.960 --> 01:23:26.460
do people who are like me, who will find this&nbsp;
interesting, where do they hang out? How can I let&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:26.460 --> 01:23:33.180
them know about it?" But I wouldn't go in — what&nbsp;
people tend to do is, the two mistakes I see in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:33.180 --> 01:23:40.500
I guess if people are trying to do what we've&nbsp;
done or we're doing, is they spread themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:40.500 --> 01:23:44.700
too thin. So they're like, "Oh, I'm going to&nbsp;
start a blog. I'm going to create a podcast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:44.700 --> 01:23:48.720
I'm going to just do an email newsletter."
And it's like, "Man, you have no idea how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:48.720 --> 01:23:54.960
work all that stuff is, good luck." And the other&nbsp;
thing that I see a lot of people doing is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:56.400 --> 01:24:01.260
thinking it's going to be easy. And so they get&nbsp;
into this trap of like, "Oh, I said something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:01.260 --> 01:24:05.160
that's really popular. I wrote an article that's&nbsp;
really popular, and now I'm going to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:05.160 --> 01:24:10.500
another one on the same thing and another one&nbsp;
on the same thing." And by the end of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:10.500 --> 01:24:16.680
it's like you're just posting the same stuff. And&nbsp;
whether that's aggressive tweets or something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:16.680 --> 01:24:23.340
you become something you wouldn't read. And so&nbsp;
I don't look at stats on the website. I look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:23.340 --> 01:24:28.020
the email stats once a week, but I never&nbsp;
look at what's the most popular article.&nbsp;

01:24:28.020 --> 01:24:33.240
I never want that to guide the next article. I&nbsp;
just really want to follow what I'm interested in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:33.240 --> 01:24:39.360
what I'm curious about, and where my energy is.&nbsp;
And I feel like that a cohort of people will find&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:39.360 --> 01:24:44.640
that and be like, "Oh, I found my tribe. This is&nbsp;
my person. I'm so glad that there's somebody else&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:44.640 --> 01:24:49.620
interested in this other than me." And that's&nbsp;
how I think about building it. But you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:49.620 --> 01:24:54.780
to find that thing for you, and I think it's&nbsp;
really, really critical that you don't spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.780 --> 01:25:00.120
yourself too thin and try to do everything.
And then you can create these systems in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:00.120 --> 01:25:04.380
life that allow you to do that, which is — let's&nbsp;
say you want to do that and you want to work full&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:04.380 --> 01:25:08.940
time at the same time, but you want to slowly&nbsp;
build up this body of content that works for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:15.240
while you're working. Well, one way to do that is&nbsp;
write every morning from 5:00 until 6:00. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:15.240 --> 01:25:18.780
you just block off the best hour of your day for&nbsp;
writing. Most people do it the other way. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:18.780 --> 01:25:23.460
block off the worst hour of their day, which might&nbsp;
be at night. And so they come home after all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:23.460 --> 01:25:30.720
baggage and all this work, and then they spend&nbsp;
all their time and effort trying to focus, instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:30.720 --> 01:25:37.260
of actually just letting it flow and writing.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I mean as far as rules go, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:37.260 --> 01:25:45.480
evergreen/timeless piece has always been important&nbsp;
to me for all the reasons that you're mentioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:45.480 --> 01:25:51.300
including the energy-giving, because if we were&nbsp;
to look at it from a different perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:51.300 --> 01:25:56.220
if you are competing for attention — and I don't&nbsp;
want to make it seem zero-sum, but if you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:56.220 --> 01:26:04.680
trying to draw people to what you are producing in&nbsp;
some capacity, if you choose timely, news-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.900 --> 01:26:12.300
topics, or people, or stuff that's getting&nbsp;
sensationalized, or stuff that's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:12.300 --> 01:26:20.820
controversial and polarizing, you are stepping&nbsp;
into the bloodiest, most crowded arena imaginable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:20.820 --> 01:26:26.640
The timeless stuff, or the evergreen stuff,&nbsp;
is hard to do. You're also capping, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:26.640 --> 01:26:33.000
a lot of your growth, frankly. But that's okay.
Shane Parrish: Well, you grow slower, but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:33.000 --> 01:26:35.445
increase the timeline you have for growth, right?
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:26:35.445 --> 01:26:39.947
Shane Parrish: The runway becomes longer even&nbsp;
if you're growing slower in the short term.&nbsp;

01:26:39.947 --> 01:26:45.480
Tim Ferriss: Exactly. You have compounding gains&nbsp;
in the form of audience growth and so on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:45.480 --> 01:26:50.520
will be more sustained as opposed to fair-weather&nbsp;
readers or listeners who are just chasing whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:50.520 --> 01:26:55.680
the algorithm serves them on any given day,&nbsp;
in any given minute. So question just on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:55.680 --> 01:27:02.700
compounding thing because Naval, of course, is&nbsp;
an incredible investor. I'd love to know a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:02.700 --> 01:27:08.040
about how you personally think about investing.
It seems like you do some private deals. Also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:08.040 --> 01:27:13.740
seems like you invest in an index fund&nbsp;
or index funds. Could you say more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:13.740 --> 01:27:19.260
that? And could you also speak to whether or&nbsp;
not you think investing indices, I guess it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:19.980 --> 01:27:27.060
has substantially changed now that, as I&nbsp;
understand it, big tech factors vary heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:27.060 --> 01:27:34.140
into at least some of the indices or indexes, I'm&nbsp;
not sure which the proper word is to use here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:34.140 --> 01:27:40.380
But how do you personally think about investing?
Shane Parrish: So I use the cash flows from Farnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:40.380 --> 01:27:47.340
Street to invest. And one of the advantages&nbsp;
to being you or me that a lot of people don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:47.340 --> 01:27:53.820
have is deal flow. So you get deal flow that&nbsp;
the best VCs in the world would be envious of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:54.660 --> 01:28:00.960
and to some extent, so do I. And so you've created&nbsp;
this vehicle that wasn't primarily for investing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:00.960 --> 01:28:05.460
but has this big benefit to it where&nbsp;
you're able to get deal flow because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:05.460 --> 01:28:09.960
of all the other things you do, not because&nbsp;
you're focused on deal flow every day. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:09.960 --> 01:28:15.780
I wanted to use that to my advantage in&nbsp;
terms of how do we get into good deals?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:15.780 --> 01:28:18.600
How do I create something sustainable?&nbsp;
How do I create something long-term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:18.600 --> 01:28:24.240
and how do I apply this knowledge that I'm having&nbsp;
fun applying? I like investing. I don't do a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:24.240 --> 01:28:30.600
of VC investing. I try to invest mostly —
In terms of check size, it's almost all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:30.600 --> 01:28:36.300
allocated to companies that are making&nbsp;
money or something that I really understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:36.300 --> 01:28:41.100
the path of. I occasionally invest with&nbsp;
friends. But when I invest with friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:41.100 --> 01:28:45.900
my rule is — go back to rules. I write that&nbsp;
investment off to zero the minute I write the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:45.900 --> 01:28:50.340
check, because I never want my investment with&nbsp;
friends to affect our friendship. So in my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:50.340 --> 01:28:54.105
it's just a zero the minute the check —
Tim Ferriss: It's a donation.&nbsp;

01:28:54.105 --> 01:28:58.080
Shane Parrish: Yeah, it's a donation to them. And&nbsp;
I'd rather be wrong supporting a friend than right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:58.080 --> 01:29:04.080
not supporting them. That doesn't do anything&nbsp;
for our friendship. And so I think about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:09.300
things that I understand and then diversifying&nbsp;
— because I don't use outside money. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:09.300 --> 01:29:15.780
no outside investors, there's Shane. And so&nbsp;
I think about how do I survive? Not how do I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:15.780 --> 01:29:21.960
maximize my returns in the short term? How&nbsp;
do I maximize my returns in the long term?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:21.960 --> 01:29:27.360
Rule number one for Warren Buffett, don't lose&nbsp;
money. Rule number two is, see rule number one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:27.360 --> 01:29:32.220
So I want to diversify when it makes sense, but&nbsp;
I also want to be in a position where I can flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:32.220 --> 01:29:39.480
to whatever makes sense. We have a real estate&nbsp;
company where we invest in real estate, but we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:39.480 --> 01:29:44.880
wait until there's a lot of distress until we put&nbsp;
more money into that, and then I can be patient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:44.880 --> 01:29:48.660
I can go years without making an investment.
Tim Ferriss: Is that mostly residential,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:48.660 --> 01:29:50.027
commercial? What kind of stuff?
Shane Parrish: All residential.&nbsp;

01:29:50.027 --> 01:29:55.620
Tim Ferriss: All residential?
Shane Parrish: Yeah. So it's multifamily at scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:55.620 --> 01:30:01.200
And so I just want to do things where there's&nbsp;
a long runway, I'm working with people I trust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:01.200 --> 01:30:08.580
the financial returns are sort of justifying the&nbsp;
investment. But also the advantage to being you or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:08.580 --> 01:30:14.100
I or this one-person team, is you can put yourself&nbsp;
in a position where you don't have to — like you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:14.100 --> 01:30:18.060
said, you stopped VC investing for years. You&nbsp;
don't have to make an investment. Nobody is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:18.060 --> 01:30:21.780
emailing you going, "Tim, why haven't you made&nbsp;
an investment?" Nobody is emailing you going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:21.780 --> 01:30:26.340
"What are you doing with my money?" All of a&nbsp;
sudden you can just let cash pile up on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:26.340 --> 01:30:31.260
balance sheet. You can just let it accumulate&nbsp;
in the bank account. And then when you see an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:31.260 --> 01:30:36.840
opportunity where the risks and rewards sort of&nbsp;
indicate that this is favorable and you have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:36.840 --> 01:30:42.600
edge in this particular investment, then you&nbsp;
can write a large check in 12 hours. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:42.600 --> 01:30:47.640
no investment committee to go through, there's&nbsp;
nothing to do, it's just you making a decision.&nbsp;

01:30:47.640 --> 01:30:53.100
And so I like that aspect of it, and I think&nbsp;
about it that way. I do the index fund thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:53.100 --> 01:30:59.400
it's sort of outside all of that, which is just&nbsp;
— I like fail safes. I don't know about you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:59.400 --> 01:31:04.980
but I hide money from myself at times. So the&nbsp;
real estate company, when I log into my bank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:04.980 --> 01:31:10.440
it doesn't even show up on the list of companies&nbsp;
we have, it's just this separate entity that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:10.440 --> 01:31:15.480
I log into once a year to get the financial&nbsp;
statements for the accountants. But mentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:15.480 --> 01:31:21.240
I've convinced myself it doesn't exist, which&nbsp;
I think is a really powerful way for me because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:22.200 --> 01:31:27.360
I don't want to convince myself that I'm&nbsp;
smarter than I am at any of this stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:32.220
and I also always want to think that I'm in&nbsp;
a worse position than I am because I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:32.220 --> 01:31:37.740
want to increase and improve my position.
Tim Ferriss: And has your thinking on index&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.740 --> 01:31:45.120
investing changed over the years or is it like I&nbsp;
see oftentimes, people saying "It's not timing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:45.120 --> 01:31:49.260
markets, it's time in the markets," so just keep&nbsp;
buying, right? Just keep buying, with respect to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:50.160 --> 01:31:55.920
an index or any number of different index&nbsp;
funds. How has your thinking remained the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:55.920 --> 01:32:00.060
same or changed with respect to index investing?
Shane Parrish: Well, the weighting of the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:00.060 --> 01:32:04.020
companies has definitely caused&nbsp;
me a little bit of hesitation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:05.760 --> 01:32:10.560
What I do with indexing is, while I invest in an&nbsp;
index fund every month, I don't always invest the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:10.560 --> 01:32:17.460
same amount of money in the index fund. So if you&nbsp;
want to think of this sort of very simplistically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:17.460 --> 01:32:25.560
when there's panic and nobody wants to invest, I&nbsp;
tend to put more money in. And when everybody's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:25.560 --> 01:32:29.460
investing and we're reaching all-time highs, I&nbsp;
tend to put less in. I don't know if that's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:29.460 --> 01:32:35.580
effective strategy for everybody else, it's just&nbsp;
sort of what I do to regulate money going into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:35.580 --> 01:32:42.120
index funds. But what we haven't seen with index&nbsp;
funds that worries me is with the rise of index&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:42.120 --> 01:32:48.720
funds, we haven't seen massive forced selling&nbsp;
of index funds and how that impacts the funds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:48.720 --> 01:32:57.480
And so I'm actually sort of counterintuitively&nbsp;
thinking the rise of activist investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:57.480 --> 01:33:02.280
they just went through this 10-year spell where&nbsp;
they didn't outperform and they're really smart.&nbsp;

01:33:02.280 --> 01:33:08.040
I'm kind of thinking, "Oh, now might be the&nbsp;
time to allocate more money into that pocket."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.760 --> 01:33:16.440
Because I think that there might be an opportunity&nbsp;
when index funds are no longer what we're thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:16.440 --> 01:33:22.920
of as index funds, and they always go up and all&nbsp;
of a sudden stock picking comes back in vogue. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.920 --> 01:33:28.380
you can buy some of them. You can buy Perishing&nbsp;
Square at — I don't know what today's price is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:28.380 --> 01:33:34.920
but a 30 percent discount to its net asset value.
And I remember doing this during COVID. So March&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:34.920 --> 01:33:41.160
2020, Bill Ackman got on TV and he's like, "I&nbsp;
have these asymmetric bets." And I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:41.160 --> 01:33:45.540
"Oh, Bill is a really smart guy. I know him."&nbsp;
So I started looking at the net asset value of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:45.540 --> 01:33:49.620
Pershing Square, and it's like the market's&nbsp;
tanking at the same time the net asset value&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:49.620 --> 01:33:54.480
is going parabolic. So I was just buying all the&nbsp;
shares I could, and that's all publicly available&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:54.480 --> 01:33:59.400
information. But you have to be in a position&nbsp;
to take advantage of that information with cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:59.400 --> 01:34:04.500
otherwise, you're for selling something to&nbsp;
do it. How do you think of an index fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:04.500 --> 01:34:08.160
investment? What do you do with your money?
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God. Yeah, I'm in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:08.160 --> 01:34:17.940
process of tremendously — I don't want to say&nbsp;
de-risking because that would presume that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:17.940 --> 01:34:23.640
accurately can identify and understand&nbsp;
all the various risks out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:23.640 --> 01:34:29.940
which kind of would be a ridiculous thing for me&nbsp;
to claim. But I have realized that many, many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.940 --> 01:34:36.600
many of my investments are highly correlated. And&nbsp;
I've always been growth first. And I have friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:36.600 --> 01:34:40.320
I'm not going to name names, but friends who&nbsp;
are like, "Look, there is no role for bonds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:40.320 --> 01:34:45.720
in your portfolio. You don't need the cash. You&nbsp;
should be 100 percent go, go, go." And these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:45.720 --> 01:34:51.300
very smart people who have done very, very well&nbsp;
over long periods of time. And intellectually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.080 --> 01:34:56.760
let's first of all say I'm not a financial&nbsp;
advisor, I do not recommend anyone take that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:56.760 --> 01:35:00.360
advice, but this is advice that a few&nbsp;
of my friends were giving to me based&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:00.360 --> 01:35:05.280
on their understanding of my life.
And what I came to realize is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:06.000 --> 01:35:15.480
while academically that might be true, it&nbsp;
actually makes it harder for me to sleep. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:15.480 --> 01:35:25.260
I am making a number of decisions right now, that&nbsp;
for anyone who is type A super-driven, successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:26.460 --> 01:35:33.000
growth-minded investor would look at askance and&nbsp;
consider probably pretty stupid. But for me, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:33.000 --> 01:35:43.200
realizing number one, I don't need to chase extra&nbsp;
gains, necessarily. I mean, I have areas where I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:43.200 --> 01:35:48.360
feel like I have informational advantage with&nbsp;
early-stage stuff. If I decided two years from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:48.360 --> 01:35:54.540
now I want to double, triple down, or 10 x down&nbsp;
on that sector, I could do that pretty easily.&nbsp;

01:35:55.620 --> 01:35:59.640
So I'm going to try and experiment for a few&nbsp;
years. I'm in the process of doing this right&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:59.640 --> 01:36:10.080
now where I'm going to be moving a lot of my cash&nbsp;
from growth positions. I can't do that in all&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:10.080 --> 01:36:15.240
cases because a lot of my stuff is locked up in&nbsp;
privates, but I'll be moving a lot from areas that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:15.240 --> 01:36:19.560
are high growth, but that are high complexity and&nbsp;
difficult for me to understand. That applies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.560 --> 01:36:27.780
a bunch of stuff overseas where I don't think the&nbsp;
world is as siloed as it once was. I mean, Nassim&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:27.780 --> 01:36:32.700
Taleb and I were talking about this recently,&nbsp;
where the idea that you're protected from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:32.700 --> 01:36:38.340
ups and downs of say, the US by being in Western&nbsp;
Europe is kind of ridiculous. And that's true even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:38.340 --> 01:36:45.960
further afield. So I'm trying to, I suppose&nbsp;
at this point, optimize for sleep. That's it.&nbsp;

01:36:45.960 --> 01:36:51.840
And I'm sure I'm going to look back a few years&nbsp;
from now and say, "Fuck, man, the opportunity cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:51.840 --> 01:36:59.520
was so high." But there are periods of time where&nbsp;
I've been red hot and I've been able to strike.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:00.180 --> 01:37:07.920
Most notably when I had much less cash&nbsp;
in sort of 2008 to 2012, let's say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:07.920 --> 01:37:15.600
which was an incredible vintage and a period for&nbsp;
startup investing. If someone were to give me my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:15.600 --> 01:37:19.440
starting capital that I had then now, even&nbsp;
adjusted for inflation, say, "Go get them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:19.440 --> 01:37:23.880
tiger," I would not succeed. I really don't think&nbsp;
I would be able to do what I did then. The world&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:23.880 --> 01:37:28.080
has just changed. The valuations and the deal&nbsp;
structuring, all of these things have changed.&nbsp;

01:37:28.680 --> 01:37:35.340
However, there have been these pockets where I've&nbsp;
been able to make good decisions only because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:35.340 --> 01:37:42.360
have extra powder. And right now I feel like I'm,&nbsp;
or at least over the last handful of years, pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:42.360 --> 01:37:49.080
fully deployed where I could get in a position&nbsp;
if I screw up my planning where I'm a forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:49.080 --> 01:37:55.380
seller or at least a distress seller. Not&nbsp;
in any kind of bet the farm way, but it adds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:55.380 --> 01:38:04.140
stress and I think that after so long and really&nbsp;
focusing so hard and for periods of time working&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:04.140 --> 01:38:10.020
so hard to get to a point where I don't need to,&nbsp;
fortunately, and there's a lot of luck involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:10.020 --> 01:38:18.300
but worry about my expenses. To manufacture&nbsp;
complicated strategies for life that add&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.300 --> 01:38:25.620
that stress back in is kind of ridiculous, it's&nbsp;
completely absurd. So I'm trying not to do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:25.620 --> 01:38:30.780
And I noticed among my friends, almost all of them&nbsp;
do this and we laugh about it. It's like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:30.780 --> 01:38:36.300
yeah, I managed to remove this stress." And they&nbsp;
figure out a really sophisticated self-sabotaging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.320 --> 01:38:43.080
subconscious way to add all that stress right back&nbsp;
in. So that's a long answer to a short question.&nbsp;

01:38:43.080 --> 01:38:46.440
Shane Parrish: Well, it's so interesting because&nbsp;
you hit on a couple things there that really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:46.440 --> 01:38:51.060
resonate with me in how I approach things. The&nbsp;
first was you were like, "The opportunity cost is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:51.060 --> 01:38:57.240
huge." Well, no, because you're only considering&nbsp;
the measurable opportunity cost. You can't measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:57.240 --> 01:38:59.846
your sleep at night as easily.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.&nbsp;

01:38:59.846 --> 01:39:00.825
Shane Parrish: Right?
Tim Ferriss: Right, right.&nbsp;

01:39:00.825 --> 01:39:07.440
Shane Parrish: And so that decision makes perfect&nbsp;
sense. Why risk what you have and don't need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:07.440 --> 01:39:12.480
what you don't have and don't want to begin&nbsp;
with? It doesn't make sense to take those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:12.480 --> 01:39:18.060
risks. When you're young, it makes sense to take&nbsp;
a lot of risk in order to sort of get ahead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:22.500
but as you get older and you have more to lose,&nbsp;
sure, you can keep pushing that button as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:22.500 --> 01:39:27.060
as you want, but at some point it doesn't&nbsp;
always have to be a pure financial decision.&nbsp;

01:39:27.060 --> 01:39:33.840
An example of that is when I left working for the&nbsp;
intelligence agency, I had this amazing pension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:33.840 --> 01:39:37.320
and they're like, "Well, starting at 55, we're&nbsp;
going to pay you this much money every year."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:37.320 --> 01:39:43.260
And it's a pretty big sum of money. I worked a lot&nbsp;
of years there and got a really decent salary. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:43.260 --> 01:39:48.300
I was like, "No, cash it in." And my accountant&nbsp;
was like, "What do you mean cash it in? That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:48.300 --> 01:39:52.620
the worst financial decision you can make." And&nbsp;
I'm like, "It's the worst financial decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:52.620 --> 01:39:58.200
but it's the best psychological decision because&nbsp;
if I know somebody's coming to save me, I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:58.200 --> 01:40:02.760
going to behave differently. I need all the chips&nbsp;
on the table in front of me and I need to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:02.760 --> 01:40:08.700
it's on me. And I know that doesn't make sense&nbsp;
to you, but for me, that's the only way that I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:08.700 --> 01:40:14.880
going to have a shot at being successful at this."
Tim Ferriss: Also, not to state the obvious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:14.880 --> 01:40:25.140
but let's go for it. Past shelter, food, all the&nbsp;
basics, and then a couple of extracurricular fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:25.140 --> 01:40:31.800
things here and there. Beyond that — even&nbsp;
for that, the extracurriculars. Presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:31.800 --> 01:40:37.620
the money is used to improve your quality of life.&nbsp;
So if you're doing things to make money that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:37.620 --> 01:40:43.653
decreasing your quality of life, it's sort of&nbsp;
antithetical to the whole name of the game —&nbsp;

01:40:43.653 --> 01:40:46.920
Shane Parrish: 100 percent.
Tim Ferriss: — in some respect. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:46.920 --> 01:40:52.260
I'm saying in part to remind myself of, because&nbsp;
making a lot of decisions right now, which for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:54.960 --> 01:41:04.020
provoke a visceral angst because I have been&nbsp;
so — during periods, I've made some terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.020 --> 01:41:07.620
decisions too, let's be very clear. I've made&nbsp;
some very terrible, very expensive decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:07.620 --> 01:41:15.540
but there have been these sprints of say, four&nbsp;
or five years where I've made a series of very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:15.540 --> 01:41:21.300
good decisions that tend to be opportunistic.
The podcast is a great example. That was nowhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:21.300 --> 01:41:27.900
in a three, five, or 10-year plan. It was very&nbsp;
opportunistic and the timing was really fortunate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:28.620 --> 01:41:35.340
And therefore, when I start moving&nbsp;
as I am right now, quite a bit into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:35.340 --> 01:41:39.540
let's just call it safer stuff. That's up for&nbsp;
debate, but let's just call it safer stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:40.200 --> 01:41:46.080
It's very counter to my conditioned way of&nbsp;
being in the world. Do you know what I mean?&nbsp;

01:41:46.080 --> 01:41:50.760
So it's an experiment. I'm also viewing it like,&nbsp;
look, yeah, I'm going to pay a bunch of hits here&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:50.760 --> 01:41:56.280
and there, and I'm blah, blah, blah, tax this,&nbsp;
tax that. Let's not let that tail wag the dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:56.280 --> 01:42:02.100
if we can avoid it. But I'm going to do it for,&nbsp;
let's just call it two years and see how I feel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:02.100 --> 01:42:07.140
right? If I'm happy I did it, great. And if I'm&nbsp;
sleeping better, great. And if I'm like, "Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.140 --> 01:42:11.340
that was an interesting experiment, but actually&nbsp;
I want to get back in the fray and roll up my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:11.340 --> 01:42:16.380
sleeves and really go at it in this full contact&nbsp;
sport known as all this early stage stuff,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:17.460 --> 01:42:20.820
then fine. It's not a permanent decision.
Shane Parrish: Yeah. I think that's an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.820 --> 01:42:26.160
way to think about it. I, mean you could also&nbsp;
— if you feel the need to keep going, you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:26.160 --> 01:42:32.220
accomplished so much and you've done really well,&nbsp;
you could also just put enough money aside that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:32.220 --> 01:42:37.680
if everything else went to zero, you'd be okay.&nbsp;
Hide that bank account from yourself, basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:37.680 --> 01:42:44.700
invest it in sort of safe, reliable stuff, and&nbsp;
then pretend it doesn't exist and then go nuts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:44.700 --> 01:42:49.140
And then with all the other stuff, you can sort&nbsp;
of play these little mind games on yourself where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:49.140 --> 01:42:55.920
it's, oh, all of a sudden I'm worth less. I've got&nbsp;
to make more. Right? And if that's your goal is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:55.920 --> 01:43:00.240
do that — but I'm totally with you in money should&nbsp;
improve the quality of life that you're living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:00.240 --> 01:43:05.520
And a lot of things that that looks like, it's&nbsp;
counterintuitive to most people because it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:05.520 --> 01:43:11.880
like time. It looks like you're buying time.
Tim Ferriss: I know we're winding to a close&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:11.880 --> 01:43:17.940
shortly, but I want to ask you perhaps just&nbsp;
broad strokes, how should people think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:17.940 --> 01:43:25.080
becoming better at making decisions? And here's&nbsp;
the backdrop for that. In the room next door, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:25.080 --> 01:43:34.500
have Poor Charlie's Almanack. I love reading about&nbsp;
mental models. However, I sometimes run into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:35.580 --> 01:43:41.760
cognitive indigestion when I try to&nbsp;
force-feed myself too many rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.760 --> 01:43:48.300
cognitive biases, fill in the blank. I get to&nbsp;
a point where I fail from too much information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:48.300 --> 01:43:52.800
not too little information. Just like if a&nbsp;
friend of mine, and this has happened a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:52.800 --> 01:43:57.960
who has no exercise habit, no controlled&nbsp;
diet to speak of, comes to me and says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:57.960 --> 01:44:01.980
"Hey, give me an index card with the&nbsp;
20 things I should do to get whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:01.980 --> 01:44:09.360
six pack abs by the end of the year." It never&nbsp;
works. It works exactly zero percent of the time.&nbsp;

01:44:09.360 --> 01:44:17.340
And I have found that sometimes I try to take&nbsp;
on too much at once. Sometimes I find looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:17.340 --> 01:44:22.980
at decisions retroactively to be helpful, but I'm&nbsp;
not sure — there are people who would say, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:22.980 --> 01:44:27.000
we don't actually learn that much from our&nbsp;
failures. I think Peter Thiel would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:27.000 --> 01:44:33.600
underscore that. How would you suggest people go&nbsp;
about getting better at making decisions? What are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:33.600 --> 01:44:39.180
some of the approaches or tools in the toolkit?
Shane Parrish: I think we hit on a couple things&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:39.180 --> 01:44:45.300
during this episode, and I'll tie this in a&nbsp;
second. So most of the time when we know we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:45.300 --> 01:44:50.520
making a decision, we tend to do fairly well in&nbsp;
that moment given the information we have because&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:50.520 --> 01:44:55.680
we're rational about the sense that — we're mostly&nbsp;
rational. We don't get it perfect, but we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.680 --> 01:45:01.620
directionally correct. If you're thinking about&nbsp;
marrying your partner, you know you're making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:01.620 --> 01:45:04.920
decision. If you're thinking about buying a&nbsp;
house, you know you're making a decision. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:04.920 --> 01:45:10.380
those moments, we tend to be more rational. And so&nbsp;
we might be wrong in the end, but we're not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:10.380 --> 01:45:15.000
wrong, especially given the information we have&nbsp;
at the time. We're generally correct. So when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.000 --> 01:45:19.560
comes back to general decision making, here's how&nbsp;
you undo yourself, you can marry the best person&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:19.560 --> 01:45:24.540
in the world for you, but if you don't invest&nbsp;
in that relationship, it all multiplies by zero.&nbsp;

01:45:24.540 --> 01:45:30.360
And investing in that relationship becomes not&nbsp;
a decision, right? Because you're not thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:30.360 --> 01:45:34.920
about it as a decision. You start living with&nbsp;
somebody and then all of a sudden you're arguing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:34.920 --> 01:45:40.920
over how you loaded the dishwasher and it&nbsp;
turns into not talking to each other. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:40.920 --> 01:45:46.860
if I tap you on the shoulder in that moment and I&nbsp;
say, "Hey, Tim, you're about to put gas or water&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:46.860 --> 01:45:52.620
onto this situation, what would you like to&nbsp;
choose?" And this is what I do with my kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:52.620 --> 01:45:56.580
and they're always like, "Oh, water."` But&nbsp;
what I'm really doing is, "Hey, you're making&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:56.580 --> 01:46:01.620
a decision to escalate here. Is that the decision&nbsp;
you want to be making?" No judgment on my part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:01.620 --> 01:46:05.580
but I'm just going to alert you to the fact&nbsp;
you're making a decision. And that tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:05.580 --> 01:46:12.360
help a lot in terms of making better decisions.
So the moments that happen in everyday life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:12.360 --> 01:46:17.220
how do we recognize that they are decisions?&nbsp;
And I would say the biggest aid to judgment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:17.220 --> 01:46:21.420
in decision-making is the position you're&nbsp;
in at the time you're making a decision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:21.420 --> 01:46:25.560
It comes back to a lot of what we talked&nbsp;
about today. There's three sort of aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:25.560 --> 01:46:29.640
to decision-making that I think about that are&nbsp;
very counterintuitive, which is the position&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:29.640 --> 01:46:34.980
you're in at the time you make the decision. Can&nbsp;
you manage your emotions? Not eliminate them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:34.980 --> 01:46:39.660
manage them. Your temperament. You don't want&nbsp;
to be making a decision when you're hungry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:39.660 --> 01:46:45.480
angry, lonely, tired. You don't want to be making&nbsp;
a decision when you're focused on proving yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:45.480 --> 01:46:50.160
right rather than getting the best outcome.
I have this phrase I remind myself with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:50.160 --> 01:46:55.080
which is "Outcome over ego." Am I focused on&nbsp;
getting the best outcome or am I focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:55.080 --> 01:46:59.520
satisfying myself and proving myself right?&nbsp;
Because the minute I'm proving myself right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:59.520 --> 01:47:05.940
what tends to happen is I ignore all this&nbsp;
information that says that I'm not right, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:05.940 --> 01:47:10.980
all of a sudden I'm not focused on the outcome.&nbsp;
So by reminding yourself to focus on the outcome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:10.980 --> 01:47:16.920
you sort of open your idea. And you get this when&nbsp;
you run a business, it tends to be a little easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:16.920 --> 01:47:23.820
in some ways because you're so tied and wed to&nbsp;
this outcome that you're looking for ideas no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:23.820 --> 01:47:28.320
matter where they come from, you're looking for&nbsp;
the best idea to get you to the outcome you want.&nbsp;

01:47:28.320 --> 01:47:33.480
And the third aspect is sort of thinking&nbsp;
independently. Are you thinking independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:33.480 --> 01:47:38.340
of the circumstances, or are the circumstances&nbsp;
thinking for you? Are you thinking independently&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:38.340 --> 01:47:42.240
of what the crowd is doing or is the crowd&nbsp;
thinking for you? Because if those are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.240 --> 01:47:46.440
happening, if circumstances or the crowd are&nbsp;
thinking for you, you're not really thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:46.440 --> 01:47:51.300
at all. But what do you have to do to think&nbsp;
independently? You have to be well-positioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:51.300 --> 01:47:55.380
so that you can think independently.
Tim Ferriss: All right. One last question&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:55.380 --> 01:48:05.400
before we land the plane, and that is if it's&nbsp;
possible, what is the short explanation for why&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:05.400 --> 01:48:09.360
we should write in the age of AI?
Shane Parrish: Writing is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:09.360 --> 01:48:12.960
process by which we realize we don't&nbsp;
understand what we're talking about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:13.860 --> 01:48:18.000
And it's only when you sit down&nbsp;
and put pen to paper or even type&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:18.960 --> 01:48:23.460
out an idea that you have, a decision you're&nbsp;
making, an idea you're wrestling with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:23.460 --> 01:48:29.040
that you sort of see where you don't understand&nbsp;
it. And the process of writing is not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.120 --> 01:48:35.520
refining that idea and helping you reflect on&nbsp;
it, but you actually generate new ideas in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:35.520 --> 01:48:40.140
process of writing. And I think it's so easy.&nbsp;
My kids do this, right? They're just like, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:40.140 --> 01:48:45.420
insert these little variables into ChatGPT, out&nbsp;
comes an essay." And I'm like, "You can't use&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:45.420 --> 01:48:50.040
that. You can feed it your essay and ask it where&nbsp;
the weak points are. That's a different way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:50.040 --> 01:48:55.200
using it than getting it to write these essays for&nbsp;
you." But the question they have is, "Why would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.200 --> 01:49:00.060
I write? This doesn't make sense. This thing&nbsp;
will do it for me and I'll get a good grade."&nbsp;

01:49:00.060 --> 01:49:05.640
And the other aspect I would say that's&nbsp;
important to writing is you learn humility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:05.640 --> 01:49:10.620
right? You learn to give up on an idea that&nbsp;
you have. You keep trying to fit this idea in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:10.620 --> 01:49:15.720
and you've probably experienced this too, and you&nbsp;
really want this idea to work. Maybe you have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:15.720 --> 01:49:19.500
great opening sentence to a paragraph, but it&nbsp;
just doesn't make sense and you can't make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:19.500 --> 01:49:23.340
make sense. So you have to give up on it, you&nbsp;
have to delete it. You literally have to delete&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:23.340 --> 01:49:27.420
something from your mind and be like, "No, that&nbsp;
doesn't make sense." "No, that's not good enough."&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:27.420 --> 01:49:33.120
And I feel like there's a certain humility&nbsp;
to that that we're almost lacking in today.&nbsp;

01:49:33.120 --> 01:49:39.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you're not going to&nbsp;
be able to examine your own thinking if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:39.240 --> 01:49:43.620
the source material is produced by ChatGPT.
Shane Parrish: Yeah. Well, what you're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:43.620 --> 01:49:48.780
doing is you have all these thoughts and you're&nbsp;
making the invisible visible. And by making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.780 --> 01:49:53.160
invisible visible, now I can see it. Now I can&nbsp;
play with it. Now I can see where it's weak and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:53.160 --> 01:49:57.600
where it's strong and how it interacts with other&nbsp;
ideas I have. But when it's just in your head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:57.600 --> 01:50:01.860
then you can't see it, so you can't reason&nbsp;
with it. You can't play with it. You just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:01.860 --> 01:50:05.280
end up playing these mental games with&nbsp;
yourself where you trick yourself into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:05.280 --> 01:50:12.060
thinking something that you don't actually think.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. I mean, I'm bullish on so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:12.660 --> 01:50:20.520
facets of AI, so I'm not a Luddite who's trying&nbsp;
to keep the horse and buggy at its pinnacle or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:20.520 --> 01:50:27.120
anything like that. However, I do think there's&nbsp;
a risk of us subjugating our own faculties in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.120 --> 01:50:33.300
same way that most of us have lost the ability to&nbsp;
navigate without Google Maps or some equivalent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:33.300 --> 01:50:43.980
If you remove your long-form thinking and remove&nbsp;
the habit of putting that down in some form such&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:43.980 --> 01:50:50.640
that you can cross-examine it and identify where&nbsp;
you're just not making any sense. Or maybe there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:50.640 --> 01:50:54.300
a shred of something that comes out spontaneously&nbsp;
that makes a whole lot of sense, but if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:54.300 --> 01:50:57.900
hadn't captured it, you would never would've&nbsp;
noticed it as it passed through your mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:59.400 --> 01:51:06.180
I can't help but think that you'll be incredibly&nbsp;
weakened over time, and if we're either getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.180 --> 01:51:11.319
stronger or weaker, I'd say directionally we&nbsp;
want to go towards the former when possible.&nbsp;

01:51:11.319 --> 01:51:14.940
Shane Parrish: Yeah, definitely. One&nbsp;
of the crazy things is writing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:14.940 --> 01:51:20.280
often the process by which we learn to think.
Tim Ferriss: Yeah. Yeah, for sure. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:20.280 --> 01:51:23.025
you're not writing down what you think,&nbsp;
you're writing in order to think.&nbsp;

01:51:23.025 --> 01:51:25.740
Shane Parrish: Right.
Tim Ferriss: And I was just journaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:25.740 --> 01:51:33.900
this morning, in fact, and one of my lines,&nbsp;
which was borrowed from some of my friends in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.900 --> 01:51:41.580
the military — super simple. I mean, this is not&nbsp;
an incredibly granular rule, but just as a guiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:41.580 --> 01:51:46.920
philosophy, "Slow is smooth, and smooth is fast."&nbsp;
Do not fucking rush, in other words. When I rush,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:46.920 --> 01:51:52.140
I make mistakes. Those mistakes cost me more time&nbsp;
in the long run, just trying to make decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:52.140 --> 01:51:58.980
quickly before defining the problem correctly.&nbsp;
It's like if you want to be fast in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:58.980 --> 01:52:05.820
it's for me at least, "Slow is smooth, and&nbsp;
smooth is fast." Shane, the new book is — yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:05.820 --> 01:52:11.520
it's a good one. Clear Thinking: Turning Ordinary&nbsp;
Moments into Extraordinary Results. People can&nbsp;&nbsp;

